[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 1: 4. c3 The Good French / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/981f96c7-7ffc-4a16-b195-81c1db90df25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Caro-Kann course! In this first chapter, we’ll dive into the Advance Variation, starting with the most common move on move 4 - 4.c3. The Caro-Kann is a defense against 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, preparing ...d5.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4, the best and most common move. We respond with 2...d5, contesting the center on the light squares.} 2...d5 3.e5 {3.e5 is played 40% of the time, and it was seen in more than 40 million games! White resolves the central tension by advancing the e-pawn, gaining space and controlling key squares in our position. We challenge this setup with 3...c5, immediately striking at the center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White reinforces the center with 4.c3, a logical and the most popular move. We continue our development while maintaining central pressure.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White develops their bishop to b5, pinning our knight to the king. It's the second most popular move, played 16% of the time. We'll force White to make a decision with their bishop by attacking it with 5...Qa5, and pin the c3-pawn to the king as well.} 5...Qa5 6.Bxc6+ {White trades the bishop for the knight, the most logical move. We recapture with 6...bxc6, and now have ideas of ...Ba6, which would prevent White from castling.} 6...bxc6 7.Bd2 {White breaks the pin by playing 7.Bd2, placing the bishop on the same diagonal as our queen.} 7...Qb6 {We calmly retreat to remove any possible discoveries. White will have trouble defending the b2- and d4-pawns. Our focus is also on completing development and getting our king to safety.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 1: 4. c3 The Good French / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/981f96c7-7ffc-4a16-b195-81c1db90df25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Caro-Kann course! In this first chapter, we’ll dive into the Advance Variation, starting with the most common move on move 4 - 4.c3. The Caro-Kann is a defense against 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, preparing ...d5.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4, the best and most common move. We respond with 2...d5, contesting the center on the light squares.} 2...d5 3.e5 {3.e5 is played 40% of the time, and it was seen in more than 40 million games! White resolves the central tension by advancing the e-pawn, gaining space and controlling key squares in our position. We challenge this setup with 3...c5, immediately striking at the center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White reinforces the center with 4.c3, a logical and the most popular move. We continue our development while maintaining central pressure.} 4...Nc6 5.Be3 {Another option is for White to play 5.Be3, a logical developing move to maintain control of the d4-pawn. In this position, we choose a slightly unusual move, 5...Nh6. We are aiming to target the dark-squared bishop by maneuvering to either g4 or f5.} 5...Nh6 6.Bxh6 {White can capture our knight, justifying giving up their dark-squared bishop to damage our structure. We simply recapture with 6...gxh6, accepting doubled pawns but gaining the bishop pair.} 6...gxh6 7.Nf3 {The most common move for White is 7.Nf3, supporting the d4-pawn. We continue developing with 7...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen.} 7...Bg4 8.Be2 {As seen in other positions, White usually breaks this pin by developing the light-squared bishop. We exchange our light-squared bishop for the knight, weakening d4.} 8...Bxf3 9.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop. We now consolidate our structure with 9...e6 and protect the c5-pawn with our bishop.} 9...e6 10.O-O {White brings their king to safety. This allows us a concrete sequence to gain the advantage, starting with 10...cxd4, creating the usual weakness on d4.} 10...cxd4 11.cxd4 {White recaptures.} 11...Qb6 {Our idea is 11...Qb6, targeting both d4 and b2. We will finish development with ...Rg8, controlling the g-file, and placing our bishop on e7. If we castle, we'll probably castle queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 1: 4. c3 The Good French / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/981f96c7-7ffc-4a16-b195-81c1db90df25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Caro-Kann course! In this first chapter, we’ll dive into the Advance Variation, starting with the most common move on move 4 - 4.c3. The Caro-Kann is a defense against 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, preparing ...d5.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4, the best and most common move. We respond with 2...d5, contesting the center on the light squares.} 2...d5 3.e5 {3.e5 is played 40% of the time, and it was seen in more than 40 million games! White resolves the central tension by advancing the e-pawn, gaining space and controlling key squares in our position. We challenge this setup with 3...c5, immediately striking at the center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White reinforces the center with 4.c3, a logical and the most popular move. We continue our development while maintaining central pressure.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {With 5.f4, White reinforces the center but neglects development, a move seen in 11% of games. Our response is to trade in the center with 5...cxd4 and then win the battle for the light squares.} 5...cxd4 6.cxd4 {White recaptures. We immediately start seizing the light squares with 6...h5, with ideas of ...Bg4 and preparing the h6-square for the knight to maneuver to f5.} 6...h5 7.Nf3 {White continues developing the knight. We play 6...Bg4, pinning the knight and following our plan.} 7...Bg4 8.Be2 {White unpins by developing the bishop, getting one step closer to castling. We close the pawn chain with 8...e6, since our bishop is already developed.} 8...e6 9.O-O {White castles their king.} 9...Nh6 {We develop the kingside knight to h6. Our plans include maneuvering it to f5, developing the dark-squared bishop, and castling. We may also develop the queen to b6 to target some of White’s weak points.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 1: 4. c3 The Good French / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/981f96c7-7ffc-4a16-b195-81c1db90df25/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Caro-Kann course! In this first chapter, we’ll dive into the Advance Variation, starting with the most common move on move 4 - 4.c3. The Caro-Kann is a defense against 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, preparing ...d5.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4, the best and most common move. We respond with 2...d5, contesting the center on the light squares.} 2...d5 3.e5 {3.e5 is played 40% of the time, and it was seen in more than 40 million games! White resolves the central tension by advancing the e-pawn, gaining space and controlling key squares in our position. We challenge this setup with 3...c5, immediately striking at the center.} 3...c5 4.c3 {White reinforces the center with 4.c3, a logical and the most popular move. We continue our development while maintaining central pressure.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {The most common move here is 5.Nf3, played in about 55% of games, developing the kingside and reinforcing central control. Our response is to soften the d4-square by exchanging in the center with 5...cxd4.} 5...cxd4 6.cxd4 {White recaptures and keeps their central pawn chain intact, but d4 has now become a target for the rest of the game. We can now activate our bishop outside the pawn chain by developing to g4 and pinning the knight with 6...Bg4.} 6...Bg4 7.Be2 {White unpins with the bishop, a natural and common continuation that also brings them closer to castling. Since our bishop is already outside the pawn chain, we can now close it with 7...e6, preparing to complete development.} 7...e6 8.O-O {White castles short, bringing their king to safety. We’re slightly behind in kingside development, so we continue with 8...Nge7, aiming for the f5-square and adding pressure to the d4-pawn.} 8...Nge7 9.Nc3 {White keeps developing, bringing the other knight to c3. We complete our knight maneuver with 9...Nf5, threatening to capture the knight and then on d4, while also freeing the e7-square for our dark-squared bishop.} 9...Nf5 10.Be3 {White spots our threat and reinforces the d4-pawn with the bishop. We continue our setup by developing our last minor piece with 10...Be7.} 10...Be7 11.a3 {White plays 11.a3, preparing b4 and preventing our knight from jumping to b4.} 11...Rc8 {We'll continue with 11...Rc8, completing our development. If h3, we'll take on f3 and play ...Bg5. We also have the thematic ideas of ...f6, a common resource in these structures to break apart the center, as well as improving the knight to c4 via a5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 2: 4. C4 Central Aggression / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/cf7901d8-fb0d-432e-baf2-fe4d5b63929b/lines"]

1.e4 {In our second study of Chapter 1, we will deal with the aggressive 4.c4. While it’s not as common as 4.c3, this move can be scary to face if you haven’t seen it before. White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro-Kann, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with both pawns by playing 2.d4. We respond with 2...d5, which is the main idea behind 1...c6.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with 3.e5, their best option, taking more space. We now counterattack the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.c4 {We’ve now reached the main idea of this second study, 4.c4, a move seen only 2% of the time. We calmly develop our knight to attack d4, forcing White to make a choice.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White’s best move is to also develop with 5.Nf3, played in about 26% of games. We respond by capturing in the center with 5...cxd4, reducing the tension before completing development.} 5...cxd4 6.Nxd4 {White recaptures the pawn. We solidify the center with 6...e6, also preparing to develop the kingside. Capturing on e5 too early would be a mistake - we’re still undeveloped, and it would allow White to seize the initiative.} 6...e6 7.Nxc6 {White can also trade knights in this position by capturing on c6. We recapture, reinforcing the center and building a solid pawn triangle.} 7...bxc6 8.cxd5 {White can continue trading in the center by capturing on d5. We’re ready for this exchange, recapturing with the c-pawn creates a passed pawn and gives us a strong center.} 8...cxd5 9.Bb5+ {The most popular move for White here is to give a check with 9.Bb5+. We simply block with our bishop, looking to exchange off our less active piece.} 9...Bd7 10.Qa4 {Instead of trading, White could increase the pressure by developing the queen to a4. However, this isn’t a good idea, it only helps us activate our pieces by sliding the rook to the b-file.} 10...Rb8 11.Bxd7+ {White now takes on d7 with check, trading bishops. We recapture with the queen.} 11...Qxd7 12.Qxd7+ {White can also capture on d7 with the queen, preventing us from castling.} 12...Kxd7 {We recapture with the king. However, with queens off the board and a pair of minor pieces traded, our king remains perfectly safe. We keep pressure on b2 and plan to maneuver the knight to c6, targeting e5.} 13.O-O {.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 2: 4. C4 Central Aggression / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/cf7901d8-fb0d-432e-baf2-fe4d5b63929b/lines"]

1.e4 {In our second study of Chapter 1, we will deal with the aggressive 4.c4. While it’s not as common as 4.c3, this move can be scary to face if you haven’t seen it before. White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro-Kann, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with both pawns by playing 2.d4. We respond with 2...d5, which is the main idea behind 1...c6.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with 3.e5, their best option, taking more space. We now counterattack the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.c4 {We’ve now reached the main idea of this second study, 4.c4, a move seen only 2% of the time. We calmly develop our knight to attack d4, forcing White to make a choice.} 4...Nc6 5.cxd5 {The capture on d5 is the most common one, chosen 46% of the time. We respond by capturing on d5 with our queen, putting pressure on White's center.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nc3 {White develops their knight to c3, attacking our queen, but leaves the d4-pawn hanging. We simply take the free pawn.} 6...Qxd4 7.Be3 {White should try to gain compensation and develop with tempo; 7.Be3 is best. We trade queens to avoid placing ours on a bad square.} 7...Qxd1+ 8.Rxd1 {White recaptures with the rook, creating the threat of Nb5-c7.} 8...Bd7 {We are behind in development so we need to be careful, that's why we develop the bishop to d7. This is prophylactic against Bb5, which would not pin the knight, and against Nb5, since it gives the rook freedom to move. Our plans include ...e6 and maneuvering the knight to f5 via h6 or e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 2: 4. C4 Central Aggression / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/cf7901d8-fb0d-432e-baf2-fe4d5b63929b/lines"]

1.e4 {In our second study of Chapter 1, we will deal with the aggressive 4.c4. While it’s not as common as 4.c3, this move can be scary to face if you haven’t seen it before. White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro-Kann, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with both pawns by playing 2.d4. We respond with 2...d5, which is the main idea behind 1...c6.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with 3.e5, their best option, taking more space. We now counterattack the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.c4 {We’ve now reached the main idea of this second study, 4.c4, a move seen only 2% of the time. We calmly develop our knight to attack d4, forcing White to make a choice.} 4...Nc6 5.cxd5 {The capture on d5 is the most common one, chosen 46% of the time. We respond by capturing on d5 with our queen, putting pressure on White's center.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nf3 {White develops their knight to f3 to support the center. We can capture a key central pawn for free!} 6...cxd4 7.Nc3 {White develops their knight to c3, a great move, using the pin on the d4-pawn to gain a tempo on our queen. We retreat our queen to its home square, and threaten to take the knight on c3.} 7...Qd8 8.Nb5 {White escapes danger with 8.Nb5, aiming to regain the pawn. We are fine allowing this, as it will take time, and instead open our dark-squared bishop with 8...e6.} 8...e6 9.Nbxd4 {White restores material equality with 9.Nbxd4. We continue developing with 9...Bb4+, gaining a tempo.} 9...Bb4+ 10.Bd2 {White blocks the check with their bishop. We trade on d2.} 10...Bxd2+ 11.Qxd2 {White recaptures with the queen. We trade again before continuing with development.} 11...Nxd4 12.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight.} 12...Ne7 {We are ready to finish development with 12...Ne7. The position is equal with a full middlegame ahead. We plan to castle short, develop our light-squared bishop to d7, and occupy the open c-file with our rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 2: 4. C4 Central Aggression / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/cf7901d8-fb0d-432e-baf2-fe4d5b63929b/lines"]

1.e4 {In our second study of Chapter 1, we will deal with the aggressive 4.c4. While it’s not as common as 4.c3, this move can be scary to face if you haven’t seen it before. White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro-Kann, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with both pawns by playing 2.d4. We respond with 2...d5, which is the main idea behind 1...c6.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with 3.e5, their best option, taking more space. We now counterattack the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.c4 {We’ve now reached the main idea of this second study, 4.c4, a move seen only 2% of the time. We calmly develop our knight to attack d4, forcing White to make a choice.} 4...Nc6 5.dxc5 {The capture on c5, played 21% of the time, does not trouble us. We respond by opening up our dark-squared bishop with 5...e6, targeting c5 while our knight continues to pressure e5.} 5...e6 6.cxd5 {White might trade in the center. We recapture with the e-pawn, maintaining pressure on both e5 and c5.} 6...exd5 7.Nf3 {White develops their knight to f3, defending the e5-pawn. The c5-pawn has been left hanging, so we capture it with our bishop, 7...Bxc5.} 7...Bxc5 8.Bb5 {White continues development and pins our knight to the king with 8.Bb5. We support our knight with 8...Nge7, moving closer to castling.} 8...Ne7 9.O-O {White castles. We do the same, bringing our king to safety.} 9...O-O 10.Nc3 {White develops naturally with 10.Nc3, pressuring on d5.} 10...Bg4 {We continue our development by pinning the knight to the queen with 10...Bg4, removing a defender of the e5-pawn. We enjoy a slight edge, with pressure on e5 and ideas of ...Re8. The passed d-pawn is also a useful asset.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 2: 4. C4 Central Aggression / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/cf7901d8-fb0d-432e-baf2-fe4d5b63929b/lines"]

1.e4 {In our second study of Chapter 1, we will deal with the aggressive 4.c4. While it’s not as common as 4.c3, this move can be scary to face if you haven’t seen it before. White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro-Kann, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with both pawns by playing 2.d4. We respond with 2...d5, which is the main idea behind 1...c6.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with 3.e5, their best option, taking more space. We now counterattack the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.c4 {We’ve now reached the main idea of this second study, 4.c4, a move seen only 2% of the time. We calmly develop our knight to attack d4, forcing White to make a choice.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White’s best move is to also develop with 5.Nf3, played in about 26% of games. We respond by capturing in the center with 5...cxd4, reducing the tension before completing development.} 5...cxd4 6.Nxd4 {White recaptures the pawn. We solidify the center with 6...e6, also preparing to develop the kingside. Capturing on e5 too early would be a mistake - we’re still undeveloped, and it would allow White to seize the initiative.} 6...e6 7.Nc3 {Continuing development is White’s best idea. We also continue developing by bringing the bishop out to c5, gaining a tempo on the knight.} 7...Bc5 8.Nxc6 {White usually captures on c6 to escape the threat. We recapture with the c-pawn, reinforcing the center and creating a solid structure.} 8...bxc6 9.cxd5 {White can continue trading in the center with 9.cxd5. This allows us to recapture with the c-pawn, opening the c-file and creating a passed pawn.} 9...cxd5 10.Bb5+ {White could check us with 10.Bb5+.} 10...Bd7 {This actually helps us develop. We block with our bishop - trading it off is fine, as it was somewhat restricted. We’ll continue with ...Ne7, O-O, and aim to create counterplay down the open b- and c-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 2: 4. C4 Central Aggression / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/cf7901d8-fb0d-432e-baf2-fe4d5b63929b/lines"]

1.e4 {In our second study of Chapter 1, we will deal with the aggressive 4.c4. While it’s not as common as 4.c3, this move can be scary to face if you haven’t seen it before. White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with the Caro-Kann, 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with both pawns by playing 2.d4. We respond with 2...d5, which is the main idea behind 1...c6.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with 3.e5, their best option, taking more space. We now counterattack the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.c4 {We’ve now reached the main idea of this second study, 4.c4, a move seen only 2% of the time. We calmly develop our knight to attack d4, forcing White to make a choice.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White’s best move is to also develop with 5.Nf3, played in about 26% of games. We respond by capturing in the center with 5...cxd4, reducing the tension before completing development.} 5...cxd4 6.Nxd4 {White recaptures the pawn. We solidify the center with 6...e6, also preparing to develop the kingside. Capturing on e5 too early would be a mistake - we’re still undeveloped, and it would allow White to seize the initiative.} 6...e6 7.Nc3 {Continuing development is White’s best idea. We also continue developing by bringing the bishop out to c5, gaining a tempo on the knight.} 7...Bc5 8.Nxc6 {White usually captures on c6 to escape the threat. We recapture with the c-pawn, reinforcing the center and creating a solid structure.} 8...bxc6 9.Qg4 {White could develop the queen to g4, a clever move that targets the g7-pawn and tries to hinder our development. We calmly develop the knight to e7, giving away the pawn since our lead in development is huge.} 9...Ne7 10.Qxg7 {White goes for it with 10.Qxg7. We respond by sliding the rook over to g8, gaining a tempo and offering yet another pawn.} 10...Rg8 11.Qxh7 {White captures the second pawn.} 11...Rb8 {We continue improving our position by bringing the other rook to the b-file. Even though we’re down two pawns, our development advantage is overwhelming, it’s actually hard for White to find a move here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 3: 4. Nf3 Natural Development / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/f64f5ed0-96de-4448-84f6-d383e49fe0f8/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the final study on Move 4 sidelines in the Advance Caro. Our focus for this chapter will be the natural 4.Nf3 - although not the most common, it’s a very logical move and one of White’s best ideas. As always, White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We build the Caro chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White pushes forward with 3.e5, entering the Advance Variation. We strike back in Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is our last move to study - an odd-looking move that keeps plenty of flexibility. It’s actually the second most popular choice, played in 18% of games. We respond by immediately taking on the center with 4...cxd4.} 4...cxd4 5.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, bringing it to the center, a move seen in 62% of games. We develop our knight to c6, offering an exchange.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {Capturing on c6 is the main move among experienced players, so White will most likely take. Naturally, we recapture.} 6...bxc6 7.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop, activating it along two diagonals and preparing to castle. We create our typical Caro light-square fortress with 7...e6, also allowing our kingside to develop.} 7...e6 8.O-O {White castles short. We continue developing our kingside with 8...Ne7, intending to reroute the knight to g6.} 8...Ne7 9.Nd2 {White also plans a knight maneuver, 9.Nd2, heading toward f3. We complete our own with 9...Ng6, targeting e5 and freeing the dark-squared bishop.} 9...Ng6 10.Nf3 {White lands the knight on f3, reinforcing the center. We play an instructive idea, preparing to exchange our light-squared bishop.} 10...a5 11.Qe2 {White anticipates our idea and prevents it with 11.Qe2, strengthening control over the f1–a6 diagonal.} 11...Be7 {We develop our dark-squared bishop to e7, getting one step closer to castling. Once our king is safe, we can consider undermining the center with ...f6, aiming to activate the dark-squared bishop. Later, we’ll activate the light-squared bishop and seize the b-file with ...Rb8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 3: 4. Nf3 Natural Development / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/f64f5ed0-96de-4448-84f6-d383e49fe0f8/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the final study on Move 4 sidelines in the Advance Caro. Our focus for this chapter will be the natural 4.Nf3 - although not the most common, it’s a very logical move and one of White’s best ideas. As always, White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We build the Caro chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White pushes forward with 3.e5, entering the Advance Variation. We strike back in Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is our last move to study - an odd-looking move that keeps plenty of flexibility. It’s actually the second most popular choice, played in 18% of games. We respond by immediately taking on the center with 4...cxd4.} 4...cxd4 5.Qxd4 {White can also capture on d4 with the queen, an unusual move that allows us to gain a tempo. We develop our knight, attacking the queen.} 5...Nc6 6.Bb5 {Pinning our knight is the most common move online. We prepare kingside development by playing 6...e6, also building a solid light-square formation.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White brings their king to safety. We support our c6 knight with 7...Ne7 and prepare ideas like ...a6 or ...Bd7 to break the pin.} 7...Ne7 8.Bg5 {White may try to keep us tied down with 8.Bg5, but this won’t work.} 8...h6 {We push White back with 8...h6 and will also play ...a6, forcing White to retreat or trade pieces. We then finish our development and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 3: 4. Nf3 Natural Development / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/f64f5ed0-96de-4448-84f6-d383e49fe0f8/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the final study on Move 4 sidelines in the Advance Caro. Our focus for this chapter will be the natural 4.Nf3 - although not the most common, it’s a very logical move and one of White’s best ideas. As always, White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We build the Caro chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White pushes forward with 3.e5, entering the Advance Variation. We strike back in Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is our last move to study - an odd-looking move that keeps plenty of flexibility. It’s actually the second most popular choice, played in 18% of games. We respond by immediately taking on the center with 4...cxd4.} 4...cxd4 5.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, bringing it to the center, a move seen in 62% of games. We develop our knight to c6, offering an exchange.} 5...Nc6 6.Bb5 {White proceeds aggressively, developing while adding pressure on our c6 knight with 6.Bb5. We simply unpin and protect the knight, leaving White with no follow-up.} 6...Bd7 7.Nxc6 {White captures on c6 with the knight. We recapture with the bishop, offering an exchange.} 7...Bxc6 8.Bxc6+ {White accepts the trade of bishops. We recapture, leaving us with a strong center.} 8...bxc6 9.O-O {White brings the king to safety with 9.O-O.} 9...e6 {Our kingside pieces need space to develop, so we give it to them with 9...e6. We simply develop our pieces to their natural squares and place the rook on the b-file. Once our development is complete, we will enjoy an edge thanks to a stronger center and an open queenside file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Advance Caro (3.e5) Move 4 Sidelines / Study 3: 4. Nf3 Natural Development / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/82d5d49d-930c-4c67-88f9-3c0a666728d8/studies/f64f5ed0-96de-4448-84f6-d383e49fe0f8/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the final study on Move 4 sidelines in the Advance Caro. Our focus for this chapter will be the natural 4.Nf3 - although not the most common, it’s a very logical move and one of White’s best ideas. As always, White starts with 1.e4, to which we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. We build the Caro chain with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White pushes forward with 3.e5, entering the Advance Variation. We strike back in Gotham style with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is our last move to study - an odd-looking move that keeps plenty of flexibility. It’s actually the second most popular choice, played in 18% of games. We respond by immediately taking on the center with 4...cxd4.} 4...cxd4 5.Qxd4 {White can also capture on d4 with the queen, an unusual move that allows us to gain a tempo. We develop our knight, attacking the queen.} 5...Nc6 6.Qf4 {White’s best option is to move the queen to a safe square. We prepare kingside development with 6...e6.} 6...e6 7.Bd3 {White develops their light-squared bishop, getting only one step away from castling.} 7...Nb4 {We conclude this variation with an important idea: targeting White’s light-squared bishop. This piece is very powerful, as it effectively controls diagonals on both sides of the board. You will usually continue kingside development in two ways: ...Ne7 for ...Nf5, or ...Nh6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 1: The Pinning Bb5 (5. Bb5 and 6. Bb5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/746ec9fd-7d76-4a43-a78e-68e87f51e37a/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second chapter of our Caro-Kann course, we’ll analyze the critical 4.dxc5 against the 3...c5 Advanced variation. Experienced players tend to prefer this critical pawn grab over the other options, as it’s objectively White’s best try. White begins with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann Defense!} 1...c6 2.d4 {White strengthens their control of the center with 2.d4. We challenge it immediately with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances the e-pawn, grabbing space and entering the Advanced Caro-Kann. We strike at the base of the pawn chain with 3...c5, the Gotham Caro approach.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with 4.dxc5, the most critical line. It's played 15% of the time. We develop our knight to c6, putting pressure on e5 and keeping our light-squared bishop open.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White pins our knight to the king 20% of the time, tactically defending the e5-pawn. We switch our focus to the c5-pawn and decide to keep our c8-bishop inside the pawn chain to avoid problems along the a4-e8 diagonal.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {White develops their bishop to protect c5, which is the best and most popular continuation, and played around 30% of the time. We play 6...Ne7, aiming for the f5 square to attack the newly developed bishop.} 6...Ne7 7.c3 {White reinforces the center with 7.c3, preparing to meet ...Nf5 with Bd4. Before jumping to f5, we unpin ourselves with 7...Bd7, a move that gives our position a lot of flexibility.} 7...Bd7 8.Bxc6 {White decides to exchange on c6, giving up the bishop for the knight. We recapture with the bishop, maintaining the idea of ...Nf5.} 8...Bxc6 9.Nf3 {White develops their knight to f3, preparing to castle. We complete our maneuver with 9...Nf5, attacking the bishop and increasing pressure on c5.} 9...Nf5 10.Bd4 {White plays 10.Bd4, anchoring the bishop in the center as planned. It seems we’re going to be down a clean pawn, but we capture the bishop with our knight.} 10...Nxd4 11.cxd4 {White recaptures with the pawn, apparently holding everything together. We strike with 11...b6, undermining the center and opening files for our pieces.} 11...b6 12.cxb6 {White captures on b6, since 12.b4 would allow ...a5! We recapture with the queen, gaining strong compensation thanks to the open lines and diagonals.} 12...Qxb6 13.Qd2 {White tries to hold on with Qd2, defending the b2-pawn. We double down on the b-file with 13...Rb8, renewing the threat.} 13...Rb8 14.b3 {White defends with b3, but this move is a losing blunder since it weakens the dark squares. We develop our bishop to b4, pinning the queen to the king.} 14...Bb4 15.Nc3 {White blocks with 15.Nc3, the only way to avoid losing the queen.} 15...Bb5 {We continue with ...Bb5, developing our light-squared bishop and preventing White from ever castling. We now have ideas of increasing the pressure on the c3-knight with moves like ...Qa5 and ...Rc8, and White’s position will soon collapse.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 1: The Pinning Bb5 (5. Bb5 and 6. Bb5) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/746ec9fd-7d76-4a43-a78e-68e87f51e37a/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second chapter of our Caro-Kann course, we’ll analyze the critical 4.dxc5 against the 3...c5 Advanced variation. Experienced players tend to prefer this critical pawn grab over the other options, as it’s objectively White’s best try. White begins with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann Defense!} 1...c6 2.d4 {White strengthens their control of the center with 2.d4. We challenge it immediately with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances the e-pawn, grabbing space and entering the Advanced Caro-Kann. We strike at the base of the pawn chain with 3...c5, the Gotham Caro approach.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with 4.dxc5, the most critical line. It's played 15% of the time. We develop our knight to c6, putting pressure on e5 and keeping our light-squared bishop open.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White defends the e5-pawn with 5.Nf3, a move we’ll explore in more depth in the next chapter. Here, we specifically look at the setup combined with 6.Bb5. This 5.Nf3 is the most popular option, played 42% of the time. We can now pin the newly developed knight with our bishop, renewing the pressure on the e5-pawn.} 5...Bg4 6.Bb5 {White counterpins our knight. We give an important check with 6...Qa5+, forcing the knight to c3 and preventing White from building the c3–b4 pawn chain that would hold their extra pawn.} 6...Qa5+ 7.Nc3 {White is forced to develop the knight to block the check. We close our pawn chain with 7...e6, defending the d5-pawn and preparing to recapture on c5 with our bishop.} 7...e6 8.Be3 {White tries to hold on to the pawn with 8.Be3. We respond with our key anti–Be3 idea: going after the bishop with 8...Ne7, intending to jump to f5.} 8...Ne7 9.a3 {White finds a good idea with 9.a3, preparing b4 to support the c5-pawn. Our response to a3 is calm, we retreat the queen to c7, staying out of b4 while adding pressure on e5. If instead 9.O-O, we would reply with ...O-O-O!, a strong move introducing ...d4 ideas.} 9...Qc7 10.h3 {White tries to unpin by attacking our light-squared bishop. We take on f3, removing the defender of the e5-pawn.} 10...Bxf3 11.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen. Our idea now is to castle long, freeing the king from the pin and creating the powerful threats of ...Nxe5 and ...d4!} 11...O-O-O 12.Qxf7 {White greedily takes a pawn that seems free... But we strike back with 12...d4!, a rook-defended pawn push that forks the bishop and knight!} 12...d4 13.Qxe6+ {White captures on e6 with check, grabbing another pawn. Both of White’s pieces, however, remain hanging.} 13...Kb8 {We calmly slide out of the check with 13...Kb8, reaching a messy position. White has taken several pawns, but we’ll win a piece in return!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 1: The Pinning Bb5 (5. Bb5 and 6. Bb5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/746ec9fd-7d76-4a43-a78e-68e87f51e37a/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second chapter of our Caro-Kann course, we’ll analyze the critical 4.dxc5 against the 3...c5 Advanced variation. Experienced players tend to prefer this critical pawn grab over the other options, as it’s objectively White’s best try. White begins with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann Defense!} 1...c6 2.d4 {White strengthens their control of the center with 2.d4. We challenge it immediately with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances the e-pawn, grabbing space and entering the Advanced Caro-Kann. We strike at the base of the pawn chain with 3...c5, the Gotham Caro approach.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with 4.dxc5, the most critical line. It's played 15% of the time. We develop our knight to c6, putting pressure on e5 and keeping our light-squared bishop open.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White pins our knight to the king 20% of the time, tactically defending the e5-pawn. We switch our focus to the c5-pawn and decide to keep our c8-bishop inside the pawn chain to avoid problems along the a4-e8 diagonal.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {White develops their bishop to protect c5, which is the best and most popular continuation, and played around 30% of the time. We play 6...Ne7, aiming for the f5 square to attack the newly developed bishop.} 6...Ne7 7.Nf3 {White develops naturally with 7.Nf3, but this move doesn’t stop our ...Nf5 plan. We go ahead and play it, attacking the bishop.} 7...Nf5 8.Bd4 {White tries to hold their position with 8.Bd4. We immediately trade with 8...Nxd4, removing the dark-squared bishop and beginning to break White’s position apart.} 8...Nxd4 9.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. After the queen recapture, we throw in a check with our own queen, setting up a tactic to regain material with a strong position.} 9...Qa5+ 10.Nc3 {White blocks the check with the knight, the only move. We capture the bishop with our queen, removing the pin on our knight.} 10...Qxb5 11.Nxb5 {White recaptures with the knight. We simply take back the queen.} 11...Nxd4 12.Nfxd4 {White takes back the piece. Now that the c5-pawn is unprotected, we capture it with 12...Bxc5. Don’t fear Nc7+ as the d4-knight will be hanging, and the knight on a8 will end up trapped!} 12...Bxc5 13.O-O-O {White castles long, defending the d4-knight. Now Nc7+ is a real threat.} 13...Ke7 {We calmly sidestep danger with 13...Ke7, keeping the better position. Soon we’ll push back with ...Bd7 or ...a6, and later bring a rook to the c-file to increase the pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 1: The Pinning Bb5 (5. Bb5 and 6. Bb5) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/746ec9fd-7d76-4a43-a78e-68e87f51e37a/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second chapter of our Caro-Kann course, we’ll analyze the critical 4.dxc5 against the 3...c5 Advanced variation. Experienced players tend to prefer this critical pawn grab over the other options, as it’s objectively White’s best try. White begins with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann Defense!} 1...c6 2.d4 {White strengthens their control of the center with 2.d4. We challenge it immediately with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances the e-pawn, grabbing space and entering the Advanced Caro-Kann. We strike at the base of the pawn chain with 3...c5, the Gotham Caro approach.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with 4.dxc5, the most critical line. It's played 15% of the time. We develop our knight to c6, putting pressure on e5 and keeping our light-squared bishop open.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White pins our knight to the king 20% of the time, tactically defending the e5-pawn. We switch our focus to the c5-pawn and decide to keep our c8-bishop inside the pawn chain to avoid problems along the a4-e8 diagonal.} 5...e6 6.Nf3 {White develops their knight to f3 instead, which is a mistake that's played about 20% of the time. White shouldn't allow easy development. White hasn’t defended c5, so we simply take it with 6...Bxc5! We also have ideas of ...Bxf2+ and ...Qb6 if White isn’t careful.} 6...Bxc5 7.O-O {Castling is logical.} 7...Ne7 {We are ready to complete development with a very comfortable position. Our e7-knight can go to g6, to attack e5, and we’ll play ...Bd7 and ...Rc8 to finish development. We’ll likely play on the queenside, although sometimes breaking with ...f6 can also give us active kingside play.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 1: The Pinning Bb5 (5. Bb5 and 6. Bb5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/746ec9fd-7d76-4a43-a78e-68e87f51e37a/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second chapter of our Caro-Kann course, we’ll analyze the critical 4.dxc5 against the 3...c5 Advanced variation. Experienced players tend to prefer this critical pawn grab over the other options, as it’s objectively White’s best try. White begins with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann Defense!} 1...c6 2.d4 {White strengthens their control of the center with 2.d4. We challenge it immediately with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances the e-pawn, grabbing space and entering the Advanced Caro-Kann. We strike at the base of the pawn chain with 3...c5, the Gotham Caro approach.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with 4.dxc5, the most critical line. It's played 15% of the time. We develop our knight to c6, putting pressure on e5 and keeping our light-squared bishop open.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White pins our knight to the king 20% of the time, tactically defending the e5-pawn. We switch our focus to the c5-pawn and decide to keep our c8-bishop inside the pawn chain to avoid problems along the a4-e8 diagonal.} 5...e6 6.Be3 {White develops their bishop to protect c5, which is the best and most popular continuation, and played around 30% of the time. We play 6...Ne7, aiming for the f5 square to attack the newly developed bishop.} 6...Ne7 7.c3 {White reinforces the center with 7.c3, preparing to meet ...Nf5 with Bd4. Before jumping to f5, we unpin ourselves with 7...Bd7, a move that gives our position a lot of flexibility.} 7...Bd7 8.Bxc6 {White decides to exchange on c6, giving up the bishop for the knight. We recapture with the bishop, maintaining the idea of ...Nf5.} 8...Bxc6 9.Nf3 {White develops their knight to f3, preparing to castle. We complete our maneuver with 9...Nf5, attacking the bishop and increasing pressure on c5.} 9...Nf5 10.Bd4 {White plays 10.Bd4, anchoring the bishop in the center as planned. It seems we’re going to be down a clean pawn, but we capture the bishop with our knight.} 10...Nxd4 11.Qxd4 {White recaptures on d4 with the queen, a less common and less obvious move than 11.cxd4, but actually a stronger one. We respond with 11...Qc7, planning that if White castles, ...Bb5 will deliver a double attack on f1 and c5.} 11...Qc7 12.b4 {White’s best move is to defend the pawn with 12.b4. We sacrifice the pawn and strike with 12...b6, aiming to open lines.} 12...b6 13.cxb6 {White captures on b6, a forced move to preserve the pawn.} 13...axb6 {We recapture, leaving ourselves a pawn down but with the bishop pair and strong queenside pressure. Our plan is simple, play ...Be7, castle, and target White’s backward c3 and a2 pawns. The two bishops guarantee us long-term compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 1: The Pinning Bb5 (5. Bb5 and 6. Bb5) / Variation 4"]
