1.e4 {In this second chapter of our Caro-Kann course, we’ll analyze the critical 4.dxc5 against the 3...c5 Advanced variation. Experienced players tend to prefer this critical pawn grab over the other options, as it’s objectively White’s best try. White begins with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann Defense!} 1...c6 2.d4 {White strengthens their control of the center with 2.d4. We challenge it immediately with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances the e-pawn, grabbing space and entering the Advanced Caro-Kann. We strike at the base of the pawn chain with 3...c5, the Gotham Caro approach.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with 4.dxc5, the most critical line. It's played 15% of the time. We develop our knight to c6, putting pressure on e5 and keeping our light-squared bishop open.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White pins our knight to the king 20% of the time, tactically defending the e5-pawn. We switch our focus to the c5-pawn and decide to keep our c8-bishop inside the pawn chain to avoid problems along the a4-e8 diagonal.} 5...e6 6.b4 {White plays 6.b4 20% of the time, a common move to protect the c5-pawn. However, it gives us a new target as well! Many players try to defend the pawn in this “natural” way, so this move appears in about 18% of games. Our solution is to unpin with 6...Bd7, creating a double attack.} 6...Bd7 7.Bxc6 {White is forced to give up the bishop for the knight to save the pawn. We recapture and already have the bishop pair advantage.} 7...Bxc6 8.Nf3 {White continues with simple development, 8.Nf3. However, this allows us to quickly strike at the overextended pawns with 8...a5, undermining the chain.} 8...a5 9.c3 {White tries to keep the pawns together with 9.c3. It’s time for us to capture on b4 first to soften up the chain.} 9...axb4 10.cxb4 {White recaptures. We now play the key move 10...b6, undermining again. White’s pawns are starting to fall apart.} 10...b6 11.Be3 {White might think they’ve defended after 11.Be3. But we still win the pawn back thanks to a small tactic that starts with 11...bxc5.} 11...bxc5 12.bxc5 {White recaptures, and it may seem they’ve managed to hold everything together.} 12...Bxc5 {Not at all! We capture on c5 with our bishop. After 13.Bxc5, we’ll play ...Qa5+ and recover the piece. We’ll then continue with ...Ne7 and ...O-O, reaching a clearly better position with at least one bishop more than Black, and plenty of open files to attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 1: The Pinning Bb5 (5. Bb5 and 6. Bb5) / Variation 7"]
