1.e4 {In this second chapter of our Caro-Kann course, we’ll analyze the critical 4.dxc5 against the 3...c5 Advanced variation. Experienced players tend to prefer this critical pawn grab over the other options, as it’s objectively White’s best try. White begins with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann Defense!} 1...c6 2.d4 {White strengthens their control of the center with 2.d4. We challenge it immediately with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances the e-pawn, grabbing space and entering the Advanced Caro-Kann. We strike at the base of the pawn chain with 3...c5, the Gotham Caro approach.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with 4.dxc5, the most critical line. It's played 15% of the time. We develop our knight to c6, putting pressure on e5 and keeping our light-squared bishop open.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White defends the e5-pawn with 5.Nf3, a move we’ll explore in more depth in the next chapter. Here, we specifically look at the setup combined with 6.Bb5. This 5.Nf3 is the most popular option, played 42% of the time. We can now pin the newly developed knight with our bishop, renewing the pressure on the e5-pawn.} 5...Bg4 6.Bb5 {White counterpins our knight. We give an important check with 6...Qa5+, forcing the knight to c3 and preventing White from building the c3–b4 pawn chain that would hold their extra pawn.} 6...Qa5+ 7.Nc3 {White is forced to develop the knight to block the check. We close our pawn chain with 7...e6, defending the d5-pawn and preparing to recapture on c5 with our bishop.} 7...e6 8.Bxc6+ {White captures on c6, removing the attacker of the e5-pawn. After our recapture 8...bxc6, we immediately double-attack the c5-pawn.} 8...bxc6 9.Be3 {White tries to defend c5 with 9.Be3. Before recapturing the pawn, we first take on f3, an important move that prevents Qd4.} 9...Bxf3 10.Qxf3 {White recaptures the piece.} 10...Bxc5 {Now it’s time for us to win back the pawn with 10...Bxc5. We’ve recovered the material with a great position, our central structure is solid and powerful. Our plan is to complete kingside development and later use the b-file for our rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 2: The Main 5. Nf3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/7e02ee85-1389-457e-bb1c-11238fc24190/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we will analyze the critical 5.Nf3, which is White’s most popular reaction in this main line. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann Defense.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. The whole point of 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so that’s what we do, challenging White’s center.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White enters the Advance Variation with 3.e5. Our usual response is the counterattacking strike 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures our pawn, a move that looks greedy but is actually the most critical. Our solution, as explained in the previous study, is to develop the knight to c6, targeting e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops with 5.Nf3, a very logical and also the most popular move, played more than 40% of the time. Our idea behind 4...Nc6 was to develop the bishop outside of the pawn chain, so we do exactly that with 5...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen.} 5...Bg4 6.c3 {Although played only 9% of the time, this is White’s best move and it forces us to be precise. It prepares b4 to defend c5, and Qa4, which can become quite dangerous against our king and bishop. We mustn't capture on e5 because of tactics on the diagonal, so we simply continue developing and renew our pressure on c5.} 6...e6 7.Be3 {The best way for White to protect the pawn is 7.Be3, a move played about 28% of the time. Our idea is to play 7...a6, a subtle but important move that lets us capture on c5 later without fear of Bb5+ checks.} 7...a6 8.Nbd2 {White’s most common reply is to continue developing with 8.Nbd2. Now we can safely take on e5, thanks to our previous move.} 8...Nxe5 9.Be2 {White unpins by developing their light-squared bishop to e2. We retreat our knight to c6, a strong square from which it controls key central squares.} 9...Nc6 10.O-O {White castles short, bringing the king to safety. We exchange on f3 before continuing development.} 10...Bxf3 11.Nxf3 {White recaptures with the knight.} 11...g6 {Our final idea is 11...g6, preparing to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop. Since our light-squared bishop has been exchanged, we place all our pawns on light squares to control both color complexes. We’ll complete development with ...Bg7, ...Nge7, and finally ...O-O, reaching a comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 2: The Main 5. Nf3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/7e02ee85-1389-457e-bb1c-11238fc24190/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we will analyze the critical 5.Nf3, which is White’s most popular reaction in this main line. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann Defense.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. The whole point of 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so that’s what we do, challenging White’s center.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White enters the Advance Variation with 3.e5. Our usual response is the counterattacking strike 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures our pawn, a move that looks greedy but is actually the most critical. Our solution, as explained in the previous study, is to develop the knight to c6, targeting e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops with 5.Nf3, a very logical and also the most popular move, played more than 40% of the time. Our idea behind 4...Nc6 was to develop the bishop outside of the pawn chain, so we do exactly that with 5...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen.} 5...Bg4 6.c3 {Although played only 9% of the time, this is White’s best move and it forces us to be precise. It prepares b4 to defend c5, and Qa4, which can become quite dangerous against our king and bishop. We mustn't capture on e5 because of tactics on the diagonal, so we simply continue developing and renew our pressure on c5.} 6...e6 7.Be3 {The best way for White to protect the pawn is 7.Be3, a move played about 28% of the time. Our idea is to play 7...a6, a subtle but important move that lets us capture on c5 later without fear of Bb5+ checks.} 7...a6 8.Nbd2 {White’s most common reply is to continue developing with 8.Nbd2. Now we can safely take on e5, thanks to our previous move.} 8...Nxe5 9.Qa4+ {White can also check, which is a move that might look scary and can sometimes cause problems, but not after we’ve played ...a6! We have only one good move here: blocking the check with our queen. It works perfectly since Bb5 is no longer possible.} 9...Qd7 10.Qxd7+ {The game could continue with White trading queens. We recapture with the knight, increasing pressure on c5.} 10...Nxd7 11.b4 {White plays 11.b4, trying to keep the c5 pawn defended.} 11...Be7 {We conclude with 11...Be7, planning a bishop maneuver to f6 to target c3. Then, we will complete development with ...Ne7 and castle, entering a complex middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 2: The Main 5. Nf3 / Variation 3"]
