1.e4 {In this study, we will analyze the critical 5.Nf3, which is White’s most popular reaction in this main line. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann Defense.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. The whole point of 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so that’s what we do, challenging White’s center.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White enters the Advance Variation with 3.e5. Our usual response is the counterattacking strike 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures our pawn, a move that looks greedy but is actually the most critical. Our solution, as explained in the previous study, is to develop the knight to c6, targeting e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops with 5.Nf3, a very logical and also the most popular move, played more than 40% of the time. Our idea behind 4...Nc6 was to develop the bishop outside of the pawn chain, so we do exactly that with 5...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen.} 5...Bg4 6.c3 {Although played only 9% of the time, this is White’s best move and it forces us to be precise. It prepares b4 to defend c5, and Qa4, which can become quite dangerous against our king and bishop. We mustn't capture on e5 because of tactics on the diagonal, so we simply continue developing and renew our pressure on c5.} 6...e6 7.Be3 {The best way for White to protect the pawn is 7.Be3, a move played about 28% of the time. Our idea is to play 7...a6, a subtle but important move that lets us capture on c5 later without fear of Bb5+ checks.} 7...a6 8.Bd4 {Another option for White is to protect the center with 8.Bd4. But here we have a strong idea to disrupt White’s plans, starting by removing a key central defender with 8...Bxf3.} 8...Bxf3 9.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen. Now it’s time for us to undermine the center with 9...b6.} 9...b6 10.cxb6 {White captures what appears to be a free pawn. We continue by taking on d4 with our knight, removing the defender of b6.} 10...Nxd4 11.cxd4 {White recaptures with the c-pawn.} 11...Qxb6 {We take on b6 with our queen. We’re temporarily a pawn down, but not for long. We can add pressure along the b-file and maneuver the knight to f5 via e7. Then, we can develop the bishop to e7 or b4 and castle short, giving us a strong, active position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 2: The Main 5. Nf3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/7e02ee85-1389-457e-bb1c-11238fc24190/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we will analyze the critical 5.Nf3, which is White’s most popular reaction in this main line. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann Defense.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. The whole point of 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so that’s what we do, challenging White’s center.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White enters the Advance Variation with 3.e5. Our usual response is the counterattacking strike 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures our pawn, a move that looks greedy but is actually the most critical. Our solution, as explained in the previous study, is to develop the knight to c6, targeting e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops with 5.Nf3, a very logical and also the most popular move, played more than 40% of the time. Our idea behind 4...Nc6 was to develop the bishop outside of the pawn chain, so we do exactly that with 5...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen.} 5...Bg4 6.c3 {Although played only 9% of the time, this is White’s best move and it forces us to be precise. It prepares b4 to defend c5, and Qa4, which can become quite dangerous against our king and bishop. We mustn't capture on e5 because of tactics on the diagonal, so we simply continue developing and renew our pressure on c5.} 6...e6 7.Bb5 {White goes for 7.Bb5, a sneaky developing move with the idea of Qa4, which would double-attack the bishop on g4 and the knight on c6. White players try this trap about 16% of the time, but we have an important intermezzo to neutralize Qa4.} 7...Bxf3 8.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen.} 8...Bxc5 {We safely regain our pawn by developing and capturing with the bishop. Our plan is simple: play ...Nge7 and ...O-O, completing development comfortably and without any problems.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 2: The Main 5. Nf3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/7e02ee85-1389-457e-bb1c-11238fc24190/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we will analyze the critical 5.Nf3, which is White’s most popular reaction in this main line. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann Defense.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. The whole point of 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so that’s what we do, challenging White’s center.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White enters the Advance Variation with 3.e5. Our usual response is the counterattacking strike 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures our pawn, a move that looks greedy but is actually the most critical. Our solution, as explained in the previous study, is to develop the knight to c6, targeting e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops with 5.Nf3, a very logical and also the most popular move, played more than 40% of the time. Our idea behind 4...Nc6 was to develop the bishop outside of the pawn chain, so we do exactly that with 5...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen.} 5...Bg4 6.c3 {Although played only 9% of the time, this is White’s best move and it forces us to be precise. It prepares b4 to defend c5, and Qa4, which can become quite dangerous against our king and bishop. We mustn't capture on e5 because of tactics on the diagonal, so we simply continue developing and renew our pressure on c5.} 6...e6 7.b4 {White builds a pawn chain to defend the loose c5-pawn with 7.b4, but this weakens the long diagonal. This move is played in about 31% of games (over ten thousand), making it the most popular choice in this position. Our plan is to shift focus toward the e5-pawn and the newly weakened long diagonal.} 7...g6 8.Bb5 {White’s best response is to develop the bishop to b5, pinning our knight and defending e5. It’s time for us to eliminate White’s central defender with 8...Bxf3.} 8...Bxf3 9.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen. We continue our fianchetto plan, attacking e5 and placing our bishop on the weakened long diagonal.} 9...Bg7 10.Bxc6+ {The game might continue with White capturing on c6, reducing pressure on e5. We’re forced to recapture with the c-pawn, maintaining our strong central structure.} 10...bxc6 11.Qe2 {White defends by placing the queen on e2. We have a very interesting resource here, sliding our queen to b8, targeting both the center and the queenside.} 11...Qb8 12.f4 {White reinforces e5 with the f-pawn. But one of the hidden ideas behind ...Qb8 was to prepare queenside play, so we strike with 12...a5!} 12...a5 13.a3 {White defends with 13.a3.} 13...g5 {We continue attacking from both sides with 13...g5! White is still up a pawn, but we’re firing at their position from all directions. Our plans include ...Ne7–g6, adding more pressure on e5, in coordination with our bishop and queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 2: The Main 5. Nf3 / Variation 6"]
