1.e4 {In this study, we will analyze the critical 5.Nf3, which is White’s most popular reaction in this main line. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann Defense.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center with 2.d4. The whole point of 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so that’s what we do, challenging White’s center.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White enters the Advance Variation with 3.e5. Our usual response is the counterattacking strike 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White captures our pawn, a move that looks greedy but is actually the most critical. Our solution, as explained in the previous study, is to develop the knight to c6, targeting e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White develops with 5.Nf3, a very logical and also the most popular move, played more than 40% of the time. Our idea behind 4...Nc6 was to develop the bishop outside of the pawn chain, so we do exactly that with 5...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen.} 5...Bg4 6.Bf4 {This bishop move, defending the e5-pawn, is a common mistake that White makes about 16% of the time. It allows us to play 6...e6, opening up our dark-squared bishop and making it easy to capture on c5.} 6...e6 7.Be2 {White has no better option than to continue developing with 7.Be2. We respond by developing and capturing at the same time. Our plan is to finish development with ...Nge7 and ...O-O, but we also have some ulterior motives like the ...Qb6 double attack if White isn’t careful.} 7...Bxc5 8.O-O {Castling might look like White’s most natural move, but it’s actually a blunder. We start by taking on f3, trading our bishop for the knight.} 8...Bxf3 9.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop.} 9...Bd4 {We move our dark-squared bishop once more, creating a double attack that wins a key pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 3: The Gotham 5. f4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/6a439f68-fbe5-4789-98de-8ef4ca2c935b/studies/b7a0c5bd-e643-4632-b21a-93e92bb5f2f4/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study of chapter 2 we are going to learn how to fight fire with fire... What happens when the Caro Course has to fight agains the e4 Course? Let's have a look! White starts the game with 1.e4 and as always we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places their second central pawn in the center of the board. Our reply is always the same, 2...d5, building the Caro Pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with the e-pawn, controlling key squares in our territory. We immediately counter the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White goes for the critical line, capturing our c5-pawn. As always, our solution is quick development with 4...Nc6, putting pressure on e5.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {Only 15% of White players know that f4 is the strongest move. Black's best response is to highlight the drawback of White's strategy, with 5...Nh6. White is mostly putting their pawns on the dark squares, so our idea will be to try to occupy the light squares.} 5...Nh6 6.c3 {White is playing only with pawns. This greedy strategy could easily backfire. White players choose this move about 17% of the time. We respond with 6...e6, creating the threat to capture on c5.} 6...e6 7.b4 {White continues playing only pawn moves and defends the c5 pawn. We immediately undermine the structure with 7...a5, putting White in trouble right away.} 7...a5 8.b5 {White may try pushing the b-pawn to attack our knight, but it doesn’t achieve much.} 8...Ne7 {We calmly retreat the knight to e7, escaping danger. White won’t be able to hold on to the extra pawn for long. Our plan is to play ...Nef5, activating the knight and opening the dark-squared bishop to target c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Advance Caro (3. e5) Weapon 1: 4...Nc6! / Study 3: The Gotham 5. f4 / Variation 7"]
