1.e4 {In this study of chapter 2 we are going to learn how to fight fire with fire... What happens when the Caro Course has to fight agains the e4 Course? Let's have a look! White starts the game with 1.e4 and as always we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places their second central pawn in the center of the board. Our reply is always the same, 2...d5, building the Caro Pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with the e-pawn, controlling key squares in our territory. We immediately counter the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White goes for the critical line, capturing our c5-pawn. As always, our solution is quick development with 4...Nc6, putting pressure on e5.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {Only 15% of White players know that f4 is the strongest move. Black's best response is to highlight the drawback of White's strategy, with 5...Nh6. White is mostly putting their pawns on the dark squares, so our idea will be to try to occupy the light squares.} 5...Nh6 6.Be3 {11% of the time, White plays right into our hands as the bishop will soon become a target for knight jumps. Our response might look crazy at first, but as we’ve seen in many lines, it’s a key concept to destabilize White’s center.} 6...g5 7.fxg5 {White captures our g-pawn, a logical reaction.} 7...Ng4 {We calmly step out of danger with 7...Ng4. White won’t be able to hold on to the extra pawn for long, and we already have a healthy lead in development.} *

