1.e4 {In this study of chapter 2 we are going to learn how to fight fire with fire... What happens when the Caro Course has to fight agains the e4 Course? Let's have a look! White starts the game with 1.e4 and as always we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places their second central pawn in the center of the board. Our reply is always the same, 2...d5, building the Caro Pawn chain.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White advances in the center with the e-pawn, controlling key squares in our territory. We immediately counter the center with 3...c5.} 3...c5 4.dxc5 {White goes for the critical line, capturing our c5-pawn. As always, our solution is quick development with 4...Nc6, putting pressure on e5.} 4...Nc6 5.f4 {Only 15% of White players know that f4 is the strongest move. Black's best response is to highlight the drawback of White's strategy, with 5...Nh6. White is mostly putting their pawns on the dark squares, so our idea will be to try to occupy the light squares.} 5...Nh6 6.c3 {White is playing only with pawns. This greedy strategy could easily backfire. White players choose this move about 17% of the time. We respond with 6...e6, creating the threat to capture on c5.} 6...e6 7.Be3 {White finally develops a piece, but the bishop on e3 will become our target. Boom! Here comes 7...g5, a key undermining strike.} 7...g5 8.fxg5 {White captures on g5, grabbing another pawn and attacking our structure. We place our knight on f5, gaining a tempo on the bishop.} 8...Nf5 9.Bf2 {White retreats the bishop to f2.} 9...Qxg5 {We recapture on g5 with the queen, recovering one pawn and putting both e5 and c5 under pressure.} *

