1.e4 {Welcome to chapter 4, where we deal with the Panov-Botvinnik Attack. This well-respected variation has fallen out of favor and popularity over the past years. Nevertheless, it was once a favorite among active and aggressive players and remains important enough to be studied. White opens with e4, and we play ...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center. We claim some central space of our own with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering what looks like the Exchange Caro. We recapture on d5, keeping symmetry for now.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {This is where the Panov truly begins. Instead of the quiet Bd3 or c3 setups, White strikes immediately with 4.c4, and it's played 22% of the time. We meet it with 4...Nf6, developing and keeping pressure on the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White continues naturally with 5.Nc3, the main move that increases pressure on d5. We respond with 5...g6, my favorite line, planning to fianchetto and castle, a setup that’s both active and safe for our king.} 5...g6 6.Qb3 {Adding pressure along the a2–f7 diagonal with 6.Qb3 is White’s most dangerous move. It's played only 8% of the time on the amateur level, although that metric increases to more than 50% on the Master level. We calmly continue with our plan, completing the fianchetto and allowing White to take on d5 if they wish.} 6...Bg7 7.cxd5 {White accepts the challenge and captures on d5, making use of the queen’s pressure. We castle, staying cool even though we’re momentarily a pawn down. The d5-pawn is doubled and isolated, and we’ll soon look to regain it.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {White develops with Be2, preparing Bf3 to reinforce d5. We bring our knight to d7, aiming for b6 to target the same pawn.} 8...Nbd7 9.Bf3 {White continues the plan with Bf3, defending d5 to the maximum. We place our knight on b6, eyeing the weak point directly.} 9...Nb6 10.Nge2 {White develops with Nge2, preparing to castle and supporting the center. We respond with 10...Bg4, trading off one of d5’s key defenders.} 10...Bg4 11.Bxg4 {White takes on g4. We recapture with the knight.} 11...Nxg4 12.a4 {White plays the thematic 12.a4, threatening a5 to dislodge our knight. We ignore it and retreat our other knight to f6, renewing pressure on d5.} 12...Nf6 13.a5 {White keeps pushing with 13.a5, trying to create chaos on the queenside. We stay calm and capture on d5, escaping the danger.} 13...Nbxd5 14.Qxb7 {White takes on b7, winning another pawn.} 14...Qd6 {We don’t panic; our activity is far more important than material here. We continue energetically with 14...Qd6, bringing the queen into play. Our pieces are about to flood the queenside, giving us tremendous compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 1: The Main Line 6. Qb3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/fb0b021f-16da-49a8-bcb0-b2a031c81556/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to chapter 4, where we deal with the Panov-Botvinnik Attack. This well-respected variation has fallen out of favor and popularity over the past years. Nevertheless, it was once a favorite among active and aggressive players and remains important enough to be studied. White opens with e4, and we play ...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White takes the center. We claim some central space of our own with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering what looks like the Exchange Caro. We recapture on d5, keeping symmetry for now.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {This is where the Panov truly begins. Instead of the quiet Bd3 or c3 setups, White strikes immediately with 4.c4, and it's played 22% of the time. We meet it with 4...Nf6, developing and keeping pressure on the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White continues naturally with 5.Nc3, the main move that increases pressure on d5. We respond with 5...g6, my favorite line, planning to fianchetto and castle, a setup that’s both active and safe for our king.} 5...g6 6.Qb3 {Adding pressure along the a2–f7 diagonal with 6.Qb3 is White’s most dangerous move. It's played only 8% of the time on the amateur level, although that metric increases to more than 50% on the Master level. We calmly continue with our plan, completing the fianchetto and allowing White to take on d5 if they wish.} 6...Bg7 7.cxd5 {White accepts the challenge and captures on d5, making use of the queen’s pressure. We castle, staying cool even though we’re momentarily a pawn down. The d5-pawn is doubled and isolated, and we’ll soon look to regain it.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {White develops with Be2, preparing Bf3 to reinforce d5. We bring our knight to d7, aiming for b6 to target the same pawn.} 8...Nbd7 9.Bf3 {White continues the plan with Bf3, defending d5 to the maximum. We place our knight on b6, eyeing the weak point directly.} 9...Nb6 10.Nge2 {White develops with Nge2, preparing to castle and supporting the center. We respond with 10...Bg4, trading off one of d5’s key defenders.} 10...Bg4 11.Bxg4 {White takes on g4. We recapture with the knight.} 11...Nxg4 12.O-O {White can also choose to castle, bringing the king to safety. This quiet move allows us to quickly regain the pawn with 12...Nf6.} 12...Nf6 13.Nf4 {White’s only way to defend the d5-pawn is by improving the knight to f4.} 13...Qd7 {We finish our setup with 13...Qd7. With ...Rfd8 coming next, we’ll win back the pawn, and the d4-pawn will soon become isolated and weak.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 2: The Straightforward 6. Cxd5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/1b62d097-16eb-4468-a3f0-b035640b69a3/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Part 2 of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack, where White plays 6.cxd5 to start an immediate confrontation in the center. As always, the game begins with 1.e4 and we respond with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White builds a strong center with both e- and d-pawns. We strike back right away with 2...d5, creating our characteristic Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange setup. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White plays 4.c4, the hallmark of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack! As in the previous study, we calmly develop with 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding support to the center. Our recipe for success remains the same: we start the fianchetto setup with 5...g6.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White captures in the center, leading to an early confrontation, a choice made in about 14% of games. Unlike in the previous chapter, we don’t hesitate and immediately recapture on d5!} 6...Nxd5 7.Nxd5 {Capturing on d5 tries to lure our queen out, but it doesn’t achieve much. We recapture with the queen, which is hard to displace and therefore very powerful.} 7...Qxd5 8.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, a reasonable developing move. We complete our fianchetto, sticking to the plan and coordinating our pieces.} 8...Bg7 9.Be3 {White brings out the dark-squared bishop to safeguard d4. We castle to safety and keep a healthy, active setup.} 9...O-O 10.Be2 {White also prepares to castle, maintaining a solid structure. We develop with purpose via 10...Nc6, and the pressure on d4 will soon be felt.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O {White gets the king to safety with short castling. We keep hammering d4 with 11...Rd8.} 11...Rd8 12.b3 {White tries 12.b3, hoping to attack our queen and gain a tempo.} 12...Qa5 {We calmly sidestep with the queen. Now ideas like ...e5 are extremely powerful thanks to the pressure along the d-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 2: The Straightforward 6. Cxd5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/1b62d097-16eb-4468-a3f0-b035640b69a3/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Part 2 of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack, where White plays 6.cxd5 to start an immediate confrontation in the center. As always, the game begins with 1.e4 and we respond with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White builds a strong center with both e- and d-pawns. We strike back right away with 2...d5, creating our characteristic Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange setup. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White plays 4.c4, the hallmark of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack! As in the previous study, we calmly develop with 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding support to the center. Our recipe for success remains the same: we start the fianchetto setup with 5...g6.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White captures in the center, leading to an early confrontation, a choice made in about 14% of games. Unlike in the previous chapter, we don’t hesitate and immediately recapture on d5!} 6...Nxd5 7.Bc4 {The best reply for White is 7.Bc4, developing with tempo. This bishop move is the main line, played in over 40% of games, and we respond by retreating our knight to b6, gaining a tempo on the bishop.} 7...Nb6 8.Bb3 {White keeps the bishop on the diagonal, maintaining pressure. We complete our fianchetto and increase the pressure on d4.} 8...Bg7 9.Nf3 {White continues developing and defends the center. We castle and prepare a simple plan: pin the f3-knight, play ...Nc6, and increase the tension around d4.} 9...O-O 10.O-O {White castles. As planned, we play 10...Nc6, directly attacking the d4 pawn.} 10...Nc6 11.d5 {White advances with d5, defending the pawn while attacking our knight. If White had played Be3 instead, we would have replied with ...Bg4.} 11...Na5 {We jump to a5, hitting the bishop on b3 and preparing active queenside play. A future knight on c4 will work perfectly with our bishop on g7 and a rook on c8.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 2: The Straightforward 6. Cxd5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/1b62d097-16eb-4468-a3f0-b035640b69a3/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Part 2 of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack, where White plays 6.cxd5 to start an immediate confrontation in the center. As always, the game begins with 1.e4 and we respond with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White builds a strong center with both e- and d-pawns. We strike back right away with 2...d5, creating our characteristic Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange setup. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White plays 4.c4, the hallmark of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack! As in the previous study, we calmly develop with 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding support to the center. Our recipe for success remains the same: we start the fianchetto setup with 5...g6.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White captures in the center, leading to an early confrontation, a choice made in about 14% of games. Unlike in the previous chapter, we don’t hesitate and immediately recapture on d5!} 6...Nxd5 7.Qb3 {The move 7.Qb3 hides a tricky idea: if we play 7...Nxc3, White has the powerful 8.Bc4! targeting f7. It’s only played about 7% of the time. We simply retreat the knight to b6 and stay solid.} 7...Nb6 8.Nf3 {Developing the knight to f3 is simply too slow. We take advantage by developing our light-squared bishop with a free tempo.} 8...Be6 9.Bb5+ {White can try to develop with check, but it’s all bark and no bite. We easily block it with 9...Nc6, improving our position while defending.} 9...Nc6 10.Qd1 {White’s queen is forced to retreat to d1.} 10...Bg7 {We complete our fianchetto, fully ready to castle. The weak d4-pawn is already under pressure, and our position practically plays itself from here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 2: The Straightforward 6. Cxd5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/1b62d097-16eb-4468-a3f0-b035640b69a3/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Part 2 of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack, where White plays 6.cxd5 to start an immediate confrontation in the center. As always, the game begins with 1.e4 and we respond with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White builds a strong center with both e- and d-pawns. We strike back right away with 2...d5, creating our characteristic Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange setup. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White plays 4.c4, the hallmark of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack! As in the previous study, we calmly develop with 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding support to the center. Our recipe for success remains the same: we start the fianchetto setup with 5...g6.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White captures in the center, leading to an early confrontation, a choice made in about 14% of games. Unlike in the previous chapter, we don’t hesitate and immediately recapture on d5!} 6...Nxd5 7.Qb3 {The move 7.Qb3 hides a tricky idea: if we play 7...Nxc3, White has the powerful 8.Bc4! targeting f7. It’s only played about 7% of the time. We simply retreat the knight to b6 and stay solid.} 7...Nb6 8.Bb5+ {White keeps up the aggression with Bb5+, checking us. We calmly block the check with our bishop.} 8...Bd7 9.Nf3 {White develops the other knight to f3, bringing more pieces into the attack. We match the pace, completing our fianchetto and preparing to castle.} 9...Bg7 10.Ne5 {White jumps to e5, threatening both mate on f7 and the bishop pair. We castle kingside, making sure we defend mate!} 10...O-O 11.Nxd7 {White captures on d7, gaining the bishop pair.} 11...N6xd7 {We can recapture either way, but I prefer taking with the b6-knight. This allows ...Nc6 next, continuing natural development.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 2: The Straightforward 6. Cxd5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/1b62d097-16eb-4468-a3f0-b035640b69a3/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Part 2 of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack, where White plays 6.cxd5 to start an immediate confrontation in the center. As always, the game begins with 1.e4 and we respond with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White builds a strong center with both e- and d-pawns. We strike back right away with 2...d5, creating our characteristic Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange setup. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White plays 4.c4, the hallmark of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack! As in the previous study, we calmly develop with 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding support to the center. Our recipe for success remains the same: we start the fianchetto setup with 5...g6.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White captures in the center, leading to an early confrontation, a choice made in about 14% of games. Unlike in the previous chapter, we don’t hesitate and immediately recapture on d5!} 6...Nxd5 7.Bb5+ {White often relies on active moves like Bc4, Qb3, or Bb5 to disturb our development. This particular idea is rare, played only about 4% of the time. We calmly meet 7.Bb5+ by blocking with our bishop, offering a trade.} 7...Bd7 8.Qb3 {White develops the queen, attacking our knight and keeping up the aggression. We don’t hesitate and immediately capture on c3.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures with the pawn, which is the best option.} 9...Bg7 {We complete our fianchetto, finishing development efficiently. Our next plan is to attack and blockade the hanging pawns on c3 and d4, turning White’s activity into long-term weaknesses.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 2: The Straightforward 6. Cxd5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/1b62d097-16eb-4468-a3f0-b035640b69a3/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Part 2 of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack, where White plays 6.cxd5 to start an immediate confrontation in the center. As always, the game begins with 1.e4 and we respond with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White builds a strong center with both e- and d-pawns. We strike back right away with 2...d5, creating our characteristic Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange setup. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White plays 4.c4, the hallmark of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack! As in the previous study, we calmly develop with 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding support to the center. Our recipe for success remains the same: we start the fianchetto setup with 5...g6.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White captures in the center, leading to an early confrontation, a choice made in about 14% of games. Unlike in the previous chapter, we don’t hesitate and immediately recapture on d5!} 6...Nxd5 7.Nf3 {White develops calmly. This gives us the perfect chance to do the same. We complete our fianchetto and plan to castle next.} 7...Bg7 8.Bc4 {White tries to bother us by attacking the knight with the bishop. We can trade on c3 here, since it leads to a specific tactical idea that works in our favor.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures. Our idea is revealed with 9...Qc7!, taking aim at the c-file.} 9...Qc7 10.Qb3 {White plays 10.Qb3, looking active for the moment. We simply castle, defending f7 and completing development without any issues.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {White castles as well, reaching what looks like a stable position. However, this is where we start to take over.} 11...Nc6 {It might seem like we’ll just continue with 11...Nc6, but the real threat is ...Na5. With ideas like ...Na5, ...Be6, and ...Rc8, we’ll create tremendous pressure on the hanging pawns at c3 and d4. The c3-pawn is weak, and the c4-square will become a perfect outpost for our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 2: The Straightforward 6. Cxd5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/1b62d097-16eb-4468-a3f0-b035640b69a3/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Part 2 of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack, where White plays 6.cxd5 to start an immediate confrontation in the center. As always, the game begins with 1.e4 and we respond with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White builds a strong center with both e- and d-pawns. We strike back right away with 2...d5, creating our characteristic Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White captures on d5, entering the Exchange setup. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White plays 4.c4, the hallmark of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack! As in the previous study, we calmly develop with 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding support to the center. Our recipe for success remains the same: we start the fianchetto setup with 5...g6.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White captures in the center, leading to an early confrontation, a choice made in about 14% of games. Unlike in the previous chapter, we don’t hesitate and immediately recapture on d5!} 6...Nxd5 7.Bc4 {The best reply for White is 7.Bc4, developing with tempo. This bishop move is the main line, played in over 40% of games, and we respond by retreating our knight to b6, gaining a tempo on the bishop.} 7...Nb6 8.Bb3 {White keeps the bishop on the diagonal, maintaining pressure. We complete our fianchetto and increase the pressure on d4.} 8...Bg7 9.d5 {White plays 9.d5 to defend, but this only strengthens our dark-squared bishop on g7. We also gain control over key dark squares like c5. We respond by castling short.} 9...O-O 10.Nf3 {White continues development with 10.Nf3, bringing another piece into play.} 10...Bg4 {Whenever we can pin the f3 knight, we should, it’s a simple but powerful idea. From here, plans like ...N8d7, and ...Rc8 give us excellent queenside play and long-term pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 3: The Meek 6. Nf3 & others / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/efc05bd2-5f5e-4c35-963c-dc5c1c2ab5e6/lines"]

1.e4 {This final study may be the least theoretical, but it’s one of the most important, because many players rely on “obvious” developing moves when they don’t know theory. That works perfectly for us, since these natural moves don’t interfere with our plans! White begins with 1.e4, no surprises there, and we respond with the trusty Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White pushes both central pawns, building a strong center. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5: you shall not pass!} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White trades in the center with 3.exd5, simplifying early. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn on d5.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White continues with 4.c4, entering the PBA. Our standard and reliable response is 4...Nf6, developing and supporting the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops as well. We stick to our usual strategy with 5...g6, preparing to fianchetto and castle.} 5...g6 6.Nf3 {White plays a natural move, the most popular in this position, appearing in nearly half of games. It lets us complete our setup comfortably. As expected, we finish with 6...Bg7, completing the fianchetto and getting ready to castle.} 6...Bg7 7.Be2 {Let's examine what happens when White plays several natural developing moves. We castle safely and soundly.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {Natural. We will put pressure on the center with our knight.} 8...Nc6 9.h3 {Preventing Bg4 ideas. We take on c4, forcing White's bishop to move again.} 9...dxc4 10.Bxc4 {The bishop recaptures. We are now playing against an IQP, and we should place our pieces to control the d5-square. Let's begin with a fianchetto.} 10...b6 11.Be3 {White adds protection to the d4-pawn. We fianchetto.} 11...Bb7 12.a3 {A common move to prevent ...Nb4-d5 ideas and to retreat the bishop on c4 if needed. We place our rook on the open c-file.} 12...Rc8 13.Ba2 {Retreating.} 13...e6 {Preparing ...Ne7 and then either ...Nf5 or ...Nd5. This will also liberate our powerful bishop on b7. Our long term strategy is to blockade and pressure the isolated d4-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 3: The Meek 6. Nf3 & others / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/efc05bd2-5f5e-4c35-963c-dc5c1c2ab5e6/lines"]

1.e4 {This final study may be the least theoretical, but it’s one of the most important, because many players rely on “obvious” developing moves when they don’t know theory. That works perfectly for us, since these natural moves don’t interfere with our plans! White begins with 1.e4, no surprises there, and we respond with the trusty Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White pushes both central pawns, building a strong center. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5: you shall not pass!} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White trades in the center with 3.exd5, simplifying early. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn on d5.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White continues with 4.c4, entering the PBA. Our standard and reliable response is 4...Nf6, developing and supporting the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops as well. We stick to our usual strategy with 5...g6, preparing to fianchetto and castle.} 5...g6 6.c5 {This pawn push might look tempting for some players, but we’re perfectly happy to see White relieve the tension in the center. It’s not a popular choice, only played in about 3% of games. Our plan stays the same: we complete the fianchetto, simple and strong.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {White continues developing with 7.Nf3, bringing another piece into play. We castle as planned.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {White prepares to castle. 8.Bd3 would have been inaccurate, as after ...Bg4 or ...Nc6, the d4- pawn becomes a real weakness. We continue naturally by developing our b-knight to its best square.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O {White castles, finishing development. We centralize with 9...Ne4, activating two pieces at once and taking the initiative.} 9...Ne4 10.Be3 {White develops and reinforces the center, thinking everything is safe. But now we strike with the tactical shot 10...Nxc5!!, sacrificing a knight to prepare ...d4 and fork two pieces.} 10...Nxc5 11.dxc5 {White is forced to capture on c5.} 11...d4 {We push d4 and regain the piece with a dominating position. Our pieces control the center, and White is left without coordination or counterplay.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 3: The Meek 6. Nf3 & others / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/efc05bd2-5f5e-4c35-963c-dc5c1c2ab5e6/lines"]

1.e4 {This final study may be the least theoretical, but it’s one of the most important, because many players rely on “obvious” developing moves when they don’t know theory. That works perfectly for us, since these natural moves don’t interfere with our plans! White begins with 1.e4, no surprises there, and we respond with the trusty Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White pushes both central pawns, building a strong center. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5: you shall not pass!} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White trades in the center with 3.exd5, simplifying early. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn on d5.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White continues with 4.c4, entering the PBA. Our standard and reliable response is 4...Nf6, developing and supporting the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops as well. We stick to our usual strategy with 5...g6, preparing to fianchetto and castle.} 5...g6 6.Bf4 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to f4, a natural-looking move, though it would be better to develop the kingside first to castle sooner. It’s played in only about 3% of games. We just stick to our plan with 6...Bg7.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf3 {The game can now take on a typical structure, where both sides develop calmly. We castle short, following simple, effective principles.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {White keeps developing and is just one move away from castling. We bring our knight out, adding pressure to the center.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O {White castles, completing development. We develop our light-squared bishop to g4, attacking a key defender of the d4-pawn.} 9...Bg4 10.h3 {White forces the pace by kicking our bishop. If instead 10.c5, we would have the strong 10...Ne4!, activating our pieces. Here we simply capture on f3, trading.} 10...Bxf3 11.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop. We take on c4, grabbing the free pawn!} 11...dxc4 12.d5 {White was counting on the counterstrike 12.d5 to attack our knight.} 12...Nb4 {We advance our knight to b4, eyeing the d3 outpost as a perfect future home.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 3: The Meek 6. Nf3 & others / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/efc05bd2-5f5e-4c35-963c-dc5c1c2ab5e6/lines"]

1.e4 {This final study may be the least theoretical, but it’s one of the most important, because many players rely on “obvious” developing moves when they don’t know theory. That works perfectly for us, since these natural moves don’t interfere with our plans! White begins with 1.e4, no surprises there, and we respond with the trusty Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White pushes both central pawns, building a strong center. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5: you shall not pass!} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White trades in the center with 3.exd5, simplifying early. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn on d5.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White continues with 4.c4, entering the PBA. Our standard and reliable response is 4...Nf6, developing and supporting the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops as well. We stick to our usual strategy with 5...g6, preparing to fianchetto and castle.} 5...g6 6.Bg5 {White can also try 6.Bg5, an interesting move that indirectly targets our d5-pawn. It’s actually the second most common choice, appearing in about 14% of games. We simply ignore the threat and continue developing, completing our fianchetto.} 6...Bg7 7.Bxf6 {White remains consistent with the plan and captures on f6. We recapture with the bishop.} 7...Bxf6 8.Nxd5 {White greedily grabs the “free” pawn, gaining a tempo on our bishop, but this tempting move is actually a mistake! We coolly retreat, and soon White will struggle to defend the d4-pawn.} 8...Bg7 9.Nf3 {White develops the other knight to defend d4. We bring out our remaining knight, steadily increasing the pressure on that same square.} 9...Nc6 10.Be2 {White develops the light-squared bishop, preparing to castle. We also castle short, completing our development.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {White castles as well, finally bringing the king to safety. We continue with 11...Bg4, attacking one of the main defenders of the d4 pawn.} 11...Bg4 12.Re1 {White plays 12.Re1, trying to indirectly pressure e7. It’s time to cash in. We capture on f3, removing the defender.} 12...Bxf3 13.Bxf3 {White recaptures, keeping material balance. We immediately push our e-pawn, hitting the knight on d5.} 13...e6 14.Nc3 {White retreats the knight.} 14...Nxd4 {Now we calmly recapture our pawn, emerging with a better structure, a dominant bishop on g7, and a powerful outpost on d4. A dream position for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Panov (3. exd5, 4. c4) / Study 3: The Meek 6. Nf3 & others / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/5359ed5a-904f-4da6-a802-55c538eff481/studies/efc05bd2-5f5e-4c35-963c-dc5c1c2ab5e6/lines"]

1.e4 {This final study may be the least theoretical, but it’s one of the most important, because many players rely on “obvious” developing moves when they don’t know theory. That works perfectly for us, since these natural moves don’t interfere with our plans! White begins with 1.e4, no surprises there, and we respond with the trusty Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White pushes both central pawns, building a strong center. We immediately challenge it with 2...d5: you shall not pass!} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White trades in the center with 3.exd5, simplifying early. We recapture with the c-pawn, keeping a pawn on d5.} 3...cxd5 4.c4 {White continues with 4.c4, entering the PBA. Our standard and reliable response is 4...Nf6, developing and supporting the center.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White develops as well. We stick to our usual strategy with 5...g6, preparing to fianchetto and castle.} 5...g6 6.Bg5 {White can also try 6.Bg5, an interesting move that indirectly targets our d5-pawn. It’s actually the second most common choice, appearing in about 14% of games. We simply ignore the threat and continue developing, completing our fianchetto.} 6...Bg7 7.Bxf6 {White remains consistent with the plan and captures on f6. We recapture with the bishop.} 7...Bxf6 8.cxd5 {Even though this move doubles White’s pawns, it’s still stronger than 8.Nxd5. We simply castle for now, enjoying the bishop pair as compensation.} 8...O-O 9.Nf3 {White continues developing soundly.} 9...e6 {We finish the variation with a creative idea, not even trying to win the pawn back, but instead sacrificing it to open the position. This allows us to activate our pieces quickly and dominate the open diagonals with our powerful bishops.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 1: The Oddball Move 5 Alternatives / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/5a5039a9-82de-4b38-a2dc-f50f3559bd56/lines"]

1.e4 {In this section, we’ll study positions where White develops a knight early on with 3.Nc3 (or 3.Nd2). We’ll introduce our first key weapon: the modern 4...Nf6 Tartakower. Though this idea has been known for decades, its real strength has only been appreciated in recent years, and even Magnus Carlsen has adopted it. White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. We strike back immediately with 2...d5, establishing our strong Caro chain.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, the Classical Variation begins as White develops to support the center, and it's played 20% of the time. Note that 3.Nd2 leads to similar play, since we’ll capture on e4 either way. We exchange in the center to gain a tempo against the knight.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. Our solution is 4...Nf6, developing rapidly even at the cost of potentially doubled pawns, as speed matters more than structure here. The best move for White is to take, but in this study we’ll focus on the alternatives.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White retreats the knight to c3, which is not a terrible move, but slightly passive. It’s rare, seen in only about 1% of games, since players usually avoid retreating. We keep developing naturally, placing our bishop on f5. Next comes ...e6 to free the other bishop.} 5...Bf5 6.Nf3 {White continues with logical development, starting with 6.Nf3. As planned, we follow up with 6...e6.} 6...e6 7.Bd3 {White develops the bishop to d3, aiming to trade off our active bishop. We don’t mind and exchange on d3 without hesitation.} 7...Bxd3 8.Qxd3 {White recaptures with the queen, keeping good central control. We continue with kingside development, 8...Be7, preparing to castle.} 8...Be7 9.O-O {White castles short. We do the same.} 9...O-O 10.Bg5 {White brings the dark-squared bishop to g5, another natural developing move.} 10...Nbd7 {We finish development with 10...Nbd7, reaching a comfortable middlegame. Our next ideas include ...Qb6 or ...Qa5 followed by rooks to e8/d8 or c8/d8. Counterplay can come from the typical ...c5 break or even ...b5 on the queenside, and we have a solid position with plenty of life left in it.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 1: The Oddball Move 5 Alternatives / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/5a5039a9-82de-4b38-a2dc-f50f3559bd56/lines"]

1.e4 {In this section, we’ll study positions where White develops a knight early on with 3.Nc3 (or 3.Nd2). We’ll introduce our first key weapon: the modern 4...Nf6 Tartakower. Though this idea has been known for decades, its real strength has only been appreciated in recent years, and even Magnus Carlsen has adopted it. White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. We strike back immediately with 2...d5, establishing our strong Caro chain.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, the Classical Variation begins as White develops to support the center, and it's played 20% of the time. Note that 3.Nd2 leads to similar play, since we’ll capture on e4 either way. We exchange in the center to gain a tempo against the knight.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. Our solution is 4...Nf6, developing rapidly even at the cost of potentially doubled pawns, as speed matters more than structure here. The best move for White is to take, but in this study we’ll focus on the alternatives.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {White can also develop the light-squared bishop to d3. At first it looks like a blunder, but surprisingly it can function as a tricky gambit, played in about 5% of games. We don’t hesitate and grab the free pawn with the queen.} 5...Qxd4 6.Nf3 {White develops the other knight with tempo, attacking our queen. We retreat to d8, safely out of harm’s way, and now we’re ready to capture on e4 to simplify the position.} 6...Qd8 7.Qe2 {White places the queen on e2, logically avoiding exchanges. We continue our development with 7...Bf5, a clever move that encourages trades.} 7...Bf5 8.Nxf6+ {White falls for the bait and captures on f6 with check, with a discovered attack on our f5-bishop. We’re forced to recapture with the g-pawn.} 8...gxf6 9.Bxf5 {White takes what appears to be a free bishop. But is it really?} 9...Qa5+ {Nope! After 9...Qa5+, we regain the piece immediately, and we come out of the opening a full pawn ahead.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 1: The Oddball Move 5 Alternatives / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/5a5039a9-82de-4b38-a2dc-f50f3559bd56/lines"]

1.e4 {In this section, we’ll study positions where White develops a knight early on with 3.Nc3 (or 3.Nd2). We’ll introduce our first key weapon: the modern 4...Nf6 Tartakower. Though this idea has been known for decades, its real strength has only been appreciated in recent years, and even Magnus Carlsen has adopted it. White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. We strike back immediately with 2...d5, establishing our strong Caro chain.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, the Classical Variation begins as White develops to support the center, and it's played 20% of the time. Note that 3.Nd2 leads to similar play, since we’ll capture on e4 either way. We exchange in the center to gain a tempo against the knight.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. Our solution is 4...Nf6, developing rapidly even at the cost of potentially doubled pawns, as speed matters more than structure here. The best move for White is to take, but in this study we’ll focus on the alternatives.} 4...Nf6 5.Ng5 {White advances the knight to g5, an aggressive-looking move, but we stay calm. This sacrifice attempt appears in about 5% of games, but it simply doesn’t work if we know our lines. We immediately counter in the center with 5...c5.} 5...c5 6.N1f3 {White develops with 6.N1f3, defending d4 and hinting at Ne5. We play 6...h6, firmly kicking away the intruding knight.} 6...h6 7.Nxf7 {The entire idea behind this line is to sacrifice on f7, but White is far from having enough development to make it work. We calmly accept the sacrifice, capturing the knight with our king.} 7...Kxf7 8.Ne5+ {White brings the remaining knight to e5 with check, trying to keep the attack going. We simply retreat the king to g8, safe and sound.} 8...Kg8 9.Bc4+ {White continues with another check, this time with the bishop, still trying to attack.} 9...e6 {We block securely with 9...e6, staying a piece up. And if White tries Ng6, we can allow the capture on h8. We’ll have two powerful pieces for the rook and a completely winning position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 1: The Oddball Move 5 Alternatives / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/5a5039a9-82de-4b38-a2dc-f50f3559bd56/lines"]

1.e4 {In this section, we’ll study positions where White develops a knight early on with 3.Nc3 (or 3.Nd2). We’ll introduce our first key weapon: the modern 4...Nf6 Tartakower. Though this idea has been known for decades, its real strength has only been appreciated in recent years, and even Magnus Carlsen has adopted it. White begins with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. We strike back immediately with 2...d5, establishing our strong Caro chain.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, the Classical Variation begins as White develops to support the center, and it's played 20% of the time. Note that 3.Nd2 leads to similar play, since we’ll capture on e4 either way. We exchange in the center to gain a tempo against the knight.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with the knight. Our solution is 4...Nf6, developing rapidly even at the cost of potentially doubled pawns, as speed matters more than structure here. The best move for White is to take, but in this study we’ll focus on the alternatives.} 4...Nf6 5.Ng3 {The retreat to g3 is the most common alternative, though the square isn’t ideal for the knight. It’s the second most popular move here, played in about 21% of games. We immediately strike with the tempting 5...h5, harassing the knight and claiming space.} 5...h5 6.h4 {White plays 6.h4, stopping us from pushing the pawn ourselves. We shift plans and counterattack on the other flank with 6...c5, hitting back in the center.} 6...c5 7.Nf3 {White develops the remaining knight and reinforces the center.} 7...Bg4 {We continue developing with 8...Bg4, pinning the f3-knight to the queen. The position is roughly equal, but we’ve achieved rapid development with active piece play. We'll continue with ...Nc6, developing behind the c-pawn, and ...e6, opening our dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, a logical but slightly inaccurate move since it’s restricted by our f6-pawn. Still, it appears in more than half of games, so we must know it well. We respond actively with 6...Bd6, preparing to castle.} 6...Bd6 7.Be2 {Developing the light-squared bishop to e2 and preparing to castle short is another logical choice for White. We continue with castling, completing our kingside development.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles into safety as well. We bring our rook to the open e-file and prepare the typical knight maneuver: Nd7-f8-g6.} 8...Re8 9.Be3 {White develops the last minor piece with 9.Be3. We stick to our plan and start rerouting the knight toward f8 and then g6.} 9...Nd7 10.c4 {White claims the center with 10.c4. Our knight heads to f8, not only preparing ...Ng6 but also opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 10...Nf8 11.Qd2 {White improves coordination with 11.Qd2, connecting the rooks. It’s time to complete our maneuver, placing the knight on the active g6-square.} 11...Ng6 12.Rad1 {White brings the a-rook to the d-file, doubling with the queen and increasing central pressure. We counter by developing our queen and forming a battery with the dark-squared bishop.} 12...Qc7 13.h3 {White plays 13.h3, stopping any ...Bg4 ideas.} 13...Nf4 {We end the variation by placing our knight on a strong, active square. Now we can capture one of White’s key bishops, winning the bishop pair. In some cases, sacrificing on h3 could also be a threat.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Bf4 {White plays 6.Bf4, a developing move that can’t be bad, but it’s not very common, appearing in only about 3% of games. We stick to our plan with 6...Bd6, offering a trade of bishops.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {White accepts the trade, exchanging our strong bishop. We recapture with the queen.} 7...Qxd6 8.c3 {We actually had a sneaky threat of ...Qb4+ to win the b2 pawn, and 8.c3 is one of the few moves that prevents it, if White spots it! We simply castle, bringing the king to safety and preparing ...Re8 or ...Rd8 to activate our rook.} 8...O-O 9.Bd3 {White develops their light-squared bishop. We continue with an interesting idea, striking in the center with 9...c5 to attack before White finishes development.} 9...c5 10.Ne2 {White develops and defends with the knight, one move away from castling. We safely capture on d4, though 10...Nc6 was also possible. We must watch for potential d-file tricks like Bxh7+!} 10...cxd4 11.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight. We develop our own knight to c6, offering a trade.} 11...Nc6 12.Nxc6 {White accepts the trade and captures on c6. We recapture with the queen, getting it off the d-file.} 12...Qxc6 13.O-O {White castles, securing the king and defending g2.} 13...Be6 {We finish development with 13...Be6, bringing out the light-squared bishop and connecting the rooks. Our active piece play compensates for the doubled pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Be3 {White plays 6.Be3, a perfectly reasonable developing move that doesn’t really change our plans. It appears in about 4% of games. We simply continue by developing our bishop to the d6-square.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White also develops smoothly, bringing out the light-squared bishop to d3, aiming at our h7 pawn. We get our king to safety by castling short.} 7...O-O 8.Ne2 {White continues development, with 8.Ne2, but our plan remains the same. We bring the rook to the open e-file and prepare our typical knight transfer.} 8...Re8 9.O-O {White castles to safety, completing development. We do the same, starting the typical maneuver with 9...Nd7.} 9...Nd7 10.c4 {White plays 10.c4, smartly taking more space and control of the center.} 10...Nf8 {We complete the maneuver by bringing the knight to f8. From here, the Nf8 can head to g6 to defend or attack via h4 or f4. The bishop on c8 can develop to e6 or g4, and we can follow with ...Qc7 and ...Rad8 to bring every piece into play.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, a logical but slightly inaccurate move since it’s restricted by our f6-pawn. Still, it appears in more than half of games, so we must know it well. We respond actively with 6...Bd6, preparing to castle.} 6...Bd6 7.Be2 {Developing the light-squared bishop to e2 and preparing to castle short is another logical choice for White. We continue with castling, completing our kingside development.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles into safety as well. We bring our rook to the open e-file and prepare the typical knight maneuver: Nd7-f8-g6.} 8...Re8 9.Be3 {White develops the last minor piece with 9.Be3. We stick to our plan and start rerouting the knight toward f8 and then g6.} 9...Nd7 10.c4 {White claims the center with 10.c4. Our knight heads to f8, not only preparing ...Ng6 but also opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 10...Nf8 11.c5 {The dubious pawn push 11.c5 has become surprisingly common. It may look tempting to attack our Bd6, but it seriously weakens the d5-square. We simply retreat the bishop to c7.} 11...Bc7 12.b4 {White tries to justify 11.c5 by launching a queenside pawn storm. We respond calmly with 12...Be6, a developing move that also targets the d5 outpost.} 12...Be6 13.a4 {White continues the advance with 13.a4. We improve our position by placing the bishop on the powerful d5-square, where it dominates the board.} 13...Bd5 14.b5 {White keeps pushing pawns on the queenside, consistent, but ineffective. Our queenside is perfectly well protected.} 14...g5 {Now it’s our turn to attack with 14...g5!, a creative and ambitious move that shows our kingside pawns aren’t just for defense. While 14...Ng6 was possible, this approach gives us extra attacking options, from the direct g4 push to slower plans like ...h6–f5–f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, a logical but slightly inaccurate move since it’s restricted by our f6-pawn. Still, it appears in more than half of games, so we must know it well. We respond actively with 6...Bd6, preparing to castle.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White plays 7.Bd3, a natural square for the bishop. We castle short, completing kingside development.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles, reaching safety. We bring our rook to the open file and prepare a thematic knight transfer toward the kingside.} 8...Re8 9.h3 {White prevents any ...Bg4 ideas with 9.h3. We begin our maneuver with 9...Nbd7, ready to reroute the knight to f8.} 9...Nd7 10.c3 {White reinforces the center with 10.c3, establishing a strong light-square structure. Our knight continues to f8, opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 10...Nf8 11.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We bring out our light-squared bishop too, with 11...Be6. Our both bishops will control key diagonals, creating a strong dual presence on the board.} 11...Be6 12.Re1 {White places the rook on the open e-file, maintaining central tension. We reposition our knight to g6, moving closer to the white king: the attack is taking shape!} 12...Ng6 13.Qc2 {White continues with a bishop–queen battery, but now it’s our turn to strike. We form a battery ourselves and set our sights on the h3-pawn with a subtle yet dangerous plan.} 13...Qd7 14.Bf1 {White wisely retreats the bishop to defend h3, but we have another idea. We improve our bishop by moving it to d5, disturbing the f3-knight.} 14...Bd5 15.Nd2 {White retreats the knight to avoid the capture. This gives us time to start pushing the f-pawn, threatening to trap the dark-squared bishop.} 15...f5 16.c4 {White tries to counter by attacking our bishop first. We respond with a brilliant desperado, capturing on g2 before advancing the f-pawn!} 16...Bxg2 17.Bxg2 {White recaptures the bishop.} 17...f4 {We regain the piece with the pawn push ...f4, keeping the attack alive and maintaining a powerful initiative against the white king.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, a logical but slightly inaccurate move since it’s restricted by our f6-pawn. Still, it appears in more than half of games, so we must know it well. We respond actively with 6...Bd6, preparing to castle.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White plays 7.Bd3, a natural square for the bishop. We castle short, completing kingside development.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles, reaching safety. We bring our rook to the open file and prepare a thematic knight transfer toward the kingside.} 8...Re8 9.h3 {White prevents any ...Bg4 ideas with 9.h3. We begin our maneuver with 9...Nbd7, ready to reroute the knight to f8.} 9...Nd7 10.c3 {White reinforces the center with 10.c3, establishing a strong light-square structure. Our knight continues to f8, opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 10...Nf8 11.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We bring out our light-squared bishop too, with 11...Be6. Our both bishops will control key diagonals, creating a strong dual presence on the board.} 11...Be6 12.Re1 {White places the rook on the open e-file, maintaining central tension. We reposition our knight to g6, moving closer to the white king: the attack is taking shape!} 12...Ng6 13.Qc2 {White continues with a bishop–queen battery, but now it’s our turn to strike. We form a battery ourselves and set our sights on the h3-pawn with a subtle yet dangerous plan.} 13...Qd7 14.Rad1 {Instead of addressing our threat, White plays another natural move, but it backfires immediately. We have a winning idea: sacrifice the bishop on h3, shattering the king’s defenses!} 14...Bxh3 15.gxh3 {White accepts the challenge and takes the bishop, but they were in trouble anyway. We capture the second pawn with the queen, bringing it dangerously close to the white king.} 15...Qxh3 16.Be2 {White tries to defend f3 by retreating the light-squared bishop to e2. The knight on f3 must stay to cover mate on h2. But it’s already too late.} 16...Nh4 {After 16...Nh4!, White is getting mated by force. Either on g2 immediately or via Qh2+ and then mate on h1. A brilliant finish that punishes natural moves with tactical precision.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, a logical but slightly inaccurate move since it’s restricted by our f6-pawn. Still, it appears in more than half of games, so we must know it well. We respond actively with 6...Bd6, preparing to castle.} 6...Bd6 7.Bc4 {White can also activate the bishop to c4, a move that prevents us from playing ...Be6 in the near future. We respond simply by castling, keeping our king safe.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles as well. Even if we don’t have the e6- square for our bishop, the g4- square is excellent, we develop there and create a strong pin.} 8...Bg4 9.h3 {White challenges the pin immediately with 9.h3. We keep the tension and preserve our active bishop with 9...Bh5, maintaining the annoying pin on the f3-knight.} 9...Bh5 10.c3 {Reinforcing the center with 10.c3 is a something White might try. We keep up our development with 10...Nbd7.} 10...Nd7 11.Re1 {White continues improving and brings the rook to the open e-file. Instead of our usual knight maneuver toward f8, we reroute it to b6, gaining a tempo on the bishop and eyeing the strong d5- square for later.} 11...Nb6 12.Bb3 {The attacked bishop retreats to b3.} 12...Bc7 {We finish with 12...Bc7, preparing the powerful ...Bc7 and ...Qd6 battery and ready to bring a rook to the e-file to contest control.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 2: 5. Nxf6 without 6. c3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/83ea6491-7568-4da2-9210-4612ba3d3916/lines"]

1.e4 {Our last study covered the alternatives on move five, but now we get down to business with 5.Nxf6, by far White’s most important option. We’ll look at all the key continuations except 6.c3, which theory considers best, though the other lines are still very common in practical play. As always, White begins with 1.e4, and we build the Caro empire with 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White occupies the center with 2.d4. The Caro empire lives on with our standard 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {White develops with 3.Nc3, entering the Classical Caro. We capture on e4, ready to attack the knight next move.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures on e4. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, 4...Nf6, developing quickly and aiming for dynamic play.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White captures on f6 by far the most often, and it's played in about 62% of games. We recapture with the e-pawn, which is a key move that accelerates development. The doubled pawns will also shield our king later on.} 5...exf6 6.Bc4 {White develops actively with 6.Bc4, preventing us from comfortably playing ...Be6 later, one of our most useful developing squares. It’s played in about 12% of games. We respond with 6...Bd6, the standard move in this line, preparing to castle quickly.} 6...Bd6 7.Qe2+ {White delivers a disruptive check with the queen! Instead, 7.Nf3 would simply transpose to familiar territory. We don’t want to retreat our bishop or move the king, so we block with the queen. Trading queens is the cleanest solution.} 7...Qe7 8.Qxe7+ {White trades queens. We recapture with the king, which temporarily steps into the open but can safely return to f8 after moving the rook.} 8...Kxe7 9.Ne2 {White continues with 9.Ne2, controlling f4 and eyeing a future Bf4 trade. We keep developing with 9...Be6, a nice idea, since if White trades, we can recapture with ...fxe6 and fix our pawn structure.} 9...Be6 10.Bd3 {White declines the trade, keeping tension on the board. If instead 10.Bb3, then ...a5! would be a nice idea.} 10...a5 {We still go for 10...a5! Not the only move, but an interesting idea to grab queenside space and prepare a potential ...Na6–Nb4 maneuver.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 3: The Main Line 6. c3: White Castles Short or Long / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/adf23c68-7008-4085-815f-d7037e48fb6d/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.Be3 {White signals an attacking race with 10.Be3, preparing to castle long. We respond with the thematic knight maneuver ...Nd7–Nf8, reinforcing the kingside.} 10...Nd7 11.O-O-O {White castles long and dreams of delivering mate on our kingside. Our knight swings across for defense with 11...Nf8, but we will soon also launch our own attack.} 11...Nf8 12.Kb1 {White plays the solid 12.Kb1, guarding a2. We develop our light-squared bishop to the active g8-a2 diagonal, preparing for queenside expansion.} 12...Be6 13.c4 {White pushes 13.c4, blocking our bishop and making it harder to play ...b5. Instead, we stay solid with 13...Rc8, preparing ...b5. Another idea was 13.h3, when we can play 13...b5. Against 14.g4 we reply with 14...h4, keeping the kingside closed.} 13...Rc8 14.Nc3 {White continues to fight against ...b5 with 14.Nc3, reinforcing the queenside.} 14...Bb4 {A nice idea, aiming to remove a defender of b5! Now if given the chance we want to trade on c3 and push ...b5. Instead if White runs away with 15. Ne4 we just retreat with ...Be7 and bring ...b5 back to the table.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 3: The Main Line 6. c3: White Castles Short or Long / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/adf23c68-7008-4085-815f-d7037e48fb6d/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.O-O {Castling short is the safer choice, but by no means worse. White avoids an attacking race and instead focuses on solid development. We continue with our h-pawn push, preventing Ng3 and keeping an eye out for potential ...h3 ideas.} 10...h4 11.h3 {Blocking out ...h3 is a logical response from White. We begin our defensive knight maneuver.} 11...Nd7 12.Be3 {White can also play 12.Be3, aiming to place a rook on the d-file. We respond with our usual move, 12...Nf8, sticking to our reliable setup.} 12...Nf8 13.Rad1 {White brings the rook to the d-file, completing development, reinforcing the center and x-raying our queen. It’s time to get our last minor piece into play, so we choose 13...Be6.} 13...Be6 14.c4 {White pushes in the center, gaining space. We mirror the idea and “x-ray” the queen with 14...Rc8.} 14...Rc8 15.Rfe1 {White continues their central buildup and places the other rook on the open e-file. Both sides are fully developed and ready for plans.} 15...Bb8 {We finish the line with a flexible move, 15...Bb8. Now White must watch out for ...Qc7 ideas and even the sharp ...g5!? to stop any piece from reaching f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 3: The Main Line 6. c3: White Castles Short or Long / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/adf23c68-7008-4085-815f-d7037e48fb6d/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.O-O {Castling short is the safer choice, but by no means worse. White avoids an attacking race and instead focuses on solid development. We continue with our h-pawn push, preventing Ng3 and keeping an eye out for potential ...h3 ideas.} 10...h4 11.h3 {Blocking out ...h3 is a logical response from White. We begin our defensive knight maneuver.} 11...Nd7 12.Bf4 {Another bishop move is 12.Bf4, a straightforward idea, as White wants to trade our annoying bishop on d6. We complete our knight maneuver with 12...Nf8, following our plan and setting up the sneaky ...Rxe2 tactic to win the e2-knight and the f4-bishop. (Rxe2 doesn't work here, because of 13.Qxe2 Bxf4 14.Qe4! when the h7 weakness proves fatal.)} 12...Nf8 13.Bxd6 {White exchanges bishops, wisely avoiding the ...Rxe2 trap. We recapture with the queen.} 13...Qxd6 14.Rfe1 {White slides the f-rook to the open file, trying to contest it.} 14...Bd7 {We also develop, ready to follow up with ...Rd8 or, even better, ...Re7–Re8 to double the rooks and take control of the file.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 3: The Main Line 6. c3: White Castles Short or Long / Variation 2"]
