1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.Be3 {White signals an attacking race with 10.Be3, preparing to castle long. We respond with the thematic knight maneuver ...Nd7–Nf8, reinforcing the kingside.} 10...Nd7 11.O-O-O {White castles long and dreams of delivering mate on our kingside. Our knight swings across for defense with 11...Nf8, but we will soon also launch our own attack.} 11...Nf8 12.Kb1 {White plays the solid 12.Kb1, guarding a2. We develop our light-squared bishop to the active g8-a2 diagonal, preparing for queenside expansion.} 12...Be6 13.Nf4 {Remember, whenever the knight lands on f4, we should capture it. So our response is 13...Bxf4, giving our bishop for the knight.} 13...Bxf4 14.Bxf4 {White recaptures.} 14...b5 {And now, push! It’s time to launch our attack with 14...b5. We are aiming to open lines toward the White king and starting the race to see who reaches the enemy king first.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 3: The Main Line 6. c3: White Castles Short or Long / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/adf23c68-7008-4085-815f-d7037e48fb6d/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.Be3 {White signals an attacking race with 10.Be3, preparing to castle long. We respond with the thematic knight maneuver ...Nd7–Nf8, reinforcing the kingside.} 10...Nd7 11.O-O-O {White castles long and dreams of delivering mate on our kingside. Our knight swings across for defense with 11...Nf8, but we will soon also launch our own attack.} 11...Nf8 12.h3 {White plays 12.h3, logically preparing a g4-push to start an attack. We keep improving our position with 12...Be6.} 12...Be6 13.g4 {White follows through with 13.g4, trying to open lines on the kingside. We counter with the clever 13...Bd5, attacking the rook and disrupting White’s attacking plans.} 13...Bd5 14.Rhg1 {The rook escapes to the g-file, hoping to use it as an attacking line. We close the queenside with 14...h4, as opening things up would only help White.} 14...h4 15.g5 {White keeps charging forward with 15.g5, still aiming to break through. If 15.c4 instead, we’d have played ...Bf3, keeping White tied up. We repeat a familiar idea, winning tempi by attacking the rook again, this time with our dark-squared bishop: 15...Bh2.} 15...Bh2 16.Rg4 {White dodges danger with 16.Rg4. But we strike once more for tempo, trapping the rook with our light-squared bishop, 16...Bf3!} 16...Bf3 17.Rxh4 {Capturing on h4 might look tempting for White. After we take on g5, the rook remains trapped with no escape squares!} 17...fxg5 18.Bh7+ {Even a check on h7 with the bishop doesn’t help. There are no discovered checks available, and the rook is simply hanging.} 18...Kh8 {After 18...Kh8, White’s attack collapses and they lose material.} *

