1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.Be3 {White signals an attacking race with 10.Be3, preparing to castle long. We respond with the thematic knight maneuver ...Nd7–Nf8, reinforcing the kingside.} 10...Nd7 11.O-O-O {White castles long and dreams of delivering mate on our kingside. Our knight swings across for defense with 11...Nf8, but we will soon also launch our own attack.} 11...Nf8 12.Nf4 {White attacks h5 by placing the knight on f4, but this only leads to a bad trade. We capture on f4, and now our upcoming ...Be6 will be incredibly strong.} 12...Bxf4 13.Bxf4 {White recaptures the piece, trying to keep control. We place our bishop on e6, a great square from which it eyes the white king directly.} 13...Be6 14.Kb1 {White slides the king to b1 to guard the a2-pawn.} 14...b5 {And now, push! It’s time to launch our attack with 14...b5. We are aiming to open lines toward the White king and starting the race to see who reaches the enemy king first.} *

