1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.O-O {Castling short is the safer choice, but by no means worse. White avoids an attacking race and instead focuses on solid development. We continue with our h-pawn push, preventing Ng3 and keeping an eye out for potential ...h3 ideas.} 10...h4 11.Nf4 {11.Nf4 is a slightly rare but very intriguing idea. White is actually playing for mate! We also improve our knight with 11...Nd7, heading to f8 for defense.} 11...Nd7 12.Qd1 {White retreats the queen to d1, returning to the home square. It’s a deep idea, though far from subtle. We bring our knight to f8, completing the defensive maneuver.} 12...Nf8 13.Qh5 {The queen jumps to h5, keeping active pressure on our kingside. We respond by capturing on f4, eliminating the troublesome knight.} 13...Bxf4 14.Bxf4 {White recaptures the piece.} 14...h3 {We finish the variation with 14...h3, softening up White’s position. The position remains highly complex.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 3: The Main Line 6. c3: White Castles Short or Long / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/adf23c68-7008-4085-815f-d7037e48fb6d/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.O-O {Castling short is the safer choice, but by no means worse. White avoids an attacking race and instead focuses on solid development. We continue with our h-pawn push, preventing Ng3 and keeping an eye out for potential ...h3 ideas.} 10...h4 11.h3 {Blocking out ...h3 is a logical response from White. We begin our defensive knight maneuver.} 11...Nd7 12.Bd2 {White plays 12.Bd2, intending to bring a rook to the e-file. We complete the plan with 12...Nf8, keeping our king perfectly safe.} 12...Nf8 13.Rae1 {This is White’s concept, bringing the a1-rook to e1 and preparing f4–f5 to gain space. We stay calm and develop with 13...Bd7, looking to connect the rooks and finishing our setup.} 13...Bd7 14.f4 {White proceeds with the aggressive 14.f4, expanding on the kingside. As the old saying goes, “attack on the flanks, counter in the center,” so we strike immediately with 14...c5!} 14...c5 15.d5 {White wisely keeps the position closed with 15.d5, obtaining a passed pawn. We respond instantly with 15...b5, aiming for queenside counterplay and preparing ...c4.} 15...b5 16.c4 {White prevents ...c4 by playing it themselves. We capture on c4, opening the b-file.} 16...bxc4 17.Bxc4 {White recaptures with the bishop, a natural move.} 17...Ng6 {We finish the variation by placing our knight on g6, reaching a rich and complex position. White’s d-pawn is passed but completely blockaded, while the White king might come under pressure in the long term. Our plan is to place the rook and queen on the b-file to exert pressure on the queenside. We also have ideas of ...f5 and ...Re8.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Classical Caro (3. Nc3/Nd2) Weapon 1: 4...Nf6! / Study 3: The Main Line 6. c3: White Castles Short or Long / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/e24d2846-d9c4-4be9-9e0e-d0d198f1ee2b/studies/adf23c68-7008-4085-815f-d7037e48fb6d/lines"]

1.e4 {The final study of the Tartakower is a big one! Here we face the theoretically critical 6.c3, a line that has sparked intense debate over the past decade among amateurs and grandmasters alike. We’ll explore a modern and dynamic approach for Black that can even lead to opposite-side castling and sharp attacking races. It all begins with 1.e4, and we answer with the Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White plays 2.d4, staking out the center. The battle for central control has begun: 2...d5 is next.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {With 3.Nc3, White reinforces the center in classical fashion. We reply with 3...dxe4, giving up some central space in exchange for quick development.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We continue with the Tartakower Variation, offering doubled pawns for fast piece activity.} 4...Nf6 5.Nxf6+ {White accepts the trade. We recapture with our e-pawn, getting ready to develop our pieces.} 5...exf6 6.c3 {The main line arises after 6.c3. White’s plan is to follow up with Bd3, Ne2, and Qc2, the most critical setup in modern theory. It’s the top choice among masters, though in under-2000 games it appears only about 17% of the time. We continue with 6...Bd6, keeping to our familiar setup.} 6...Bd6 7.Bd3 {White develops with Bd3, placing the bishop on its strongest square. We castle short, following standard Tartakower development.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White continues with the subtle 8.Qc2, forming a battery towards h7 and putting direct pressure on our kingside. We develop with tempo, playing 8...Re8+.} 8...Re8+ 9.Ne2 {A key difference in this line is that White avoids Nf3, preferring to retain the options given by deploying the knight to e2. Our response is the revolutionary 9...h5!, a brilliant improvement over the older 9...g6 or 9...h6, both of which gave White attacking chances. The h-pawn will now become a weapon in our kingside play, especially if White castles short.} 9...h5 10.O-O {Castling short is the safer choice, but by no means worse. White avoids an attacking race and instead focuses on solid development. We continue with our h-pawn push, preventing Ng3 and keeping an eye out for potential ...h3 ideas.} 10...h4 11.Bf4 {White tries to trade our strong bishop.} 11...Na6 {11...Na6 is an interesting solution since we can’t go for ...Nd7. The knight will reroute into the game via c7, then e6 or d5, reaching great squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.exd5 {Taking on d5 is a move reminiscent of the Exchange Caro-Kann and is actually the second most popular choice, appearing in about 25% of games. Players love to capture in the center and relieve the tension. We recapture with the e-pawn, maintaining a strong pawn chain with a majority of pawns on light squares.} 6...cxd5 7.d4 {White takes the center with 7.d4. We develop our knight to c6, attacking d4. Our plan is simple: continue with ...Nf6, ...Be7, ...O-O, and look for counterplay on the queenside.} 7...Nc6 8.Bb5 {White can continue by pinning our knight to the king. We respond by sliding the rook to the semi-open c-file, both supporting the knight and increasing our activity.} 8...Rc8 9.O-O {White castles short, a logical developing move. We now play 9...a6, aiming to chase the bishop away so we can capture the d4 pawn.} 9...a6 10.Bxc6+ {White exchanges on c6, a necessary capture to maintain material balance. We recapture with the rook, and our plan is to complete kingside development and prepare to castle.} 10...Rxc6 11.Re1 {White lines up their rook with our king, creating potential threats. We resolve the issue by developing our bishop, pinning the knight to the rook.} 11...Bb4 12.Bd2 {White develops their bishop to d2, supporting the knight and removing the pin.} 12...Nf6 {We continue our kingside development, while maintaining strong pressure along the open c-file. Our next moves will likely be ...O-O and ...Qc7, adding even more pressure down the file.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.d4 {Taking over the center with 6.d4 is White’s most natural and common move (played about 32% of the time). We counter this by developing with 6...Nf6, putting pressure on the center and keeping an eye on e4 and d5.} 6...Nf6 7.e5 {White gains space by advancing the e-pawn, also gaining a tempo on our knight. We escape with 7...Nfd7, preparing to counterattack soon with ...c5, although sometimes we’ll first include the preparatory ...a6.} 7...Nfd7 8.Bd3 {White’s most common move is the natural 8.Bd3, but it doesn’t address our upcoming ...c5 break. So that’s exactly what we play, 8...c5, creating counterplay and freeing the c6 square for our knight.} 8...c5 9.Be3 {White can develop their dark-squared bishop to e3 to try to hold the position. We continue developing with 9...Nc6, pressuring d4 and preventing White from capturing, since ...d4 would win a piece.} 9...Nc6 10.Ne2 {White might think they’re out of trouble after 10.Ne2, defending d4 and avoiding any fork ideas. But...} 10...c4 {We’re just in time to trap White’s bishop! We leave the opening with a full extra piece and a completely winning position.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.d4 {Taking over the center with 6.d4 is White’s most natural and common move (played about 32% of the time). We counter this by developing with 6...Nf6, putting pressure on the center and keeping an eye on e4 and d5.} 6...Nf6 7.e5 {White gains space by advancing the e-pawn, also gaining a tempo on our knight. We escape with 7...Nfd7, preparing to counterattack soon with ...c5, although sometimes we’ll first include the preparatory ...a6.} 7...Nfd7 8.Ne2 {White moves the knight back to e2 to allow the c3-push, defending d4 and preparing for ...c5. We strike anyway with 8...c5, challenging the center and freeing the c6 square for our b-knight.} 8...c5 9.c3 {White plays 9.c3, building a long pawn chain on dark squares. We keep the tension and continue developing, while maintaining pressure on White’s center.} 9...Nc6 10.Be3 {White tries to hold the position together by developing the bishop to e3, defending the d4-pawn. However, the queenside starts to collapse. We begin by capturing on d4.} 10...cxd4 11.cxd4 {White recaptures with the c-pawn, keeping e5 protected.} 11...Qa5+ {Now it’s time for a powerful check with 11...Qa5+! Both Nc3 or Bd2 can be met with ...Bb4, leaving White struggling to hold the weak d4, c3, and b2 squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {This bishop move, 6.Be2, is the main line at top-level play, even though it’s only played about 7% of the time below 2000 ELO. I’m a big fan of our solution here, a fianchetto approach. Most players aren’t aware of the best way to defend against it.} 6...g6 7.d4 {Playing 7.d4 is a stronger move, immediately blocking the dark-squared main diagonal. We continue with 7...Bg7, developing with tempo against the d4 pawn.} 7...Bg7 8.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to e3, defending the pawn. We keep developing with 8...Nf6, adding pressure to White’s center.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O-O {White can try the ambitious plan of castling long. Our reply is to capture on e4, going for simplification. If instead 9.e5, then ...Nfd7 followed by ...a6 and ...c5 would have given us counterplay.} 9...Nxe4 10.Nxe4 {White captures on e4. We recapture with the d-pawn.} 10...dxe4 11.Qxe4 {White recaptures with the queen.} 11...Qd5 {We end the variation with 11...Qd5, forcing the trade of queens. Our next moves will be ...cxd5, ...Nc6, and later ...Rc8, reaching a balanced and solid position.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.g3 {Another option for White is 6.g3, aiming to fianchetto the light-squared bishop, played only about 5% of the time. Our pawn chain easily blocks that bishop, so there’s no need to worry. We respond with ...g6, going for our own fianchetto and placing more pawns on light squares.} 6...g6 7.d4 {Instead of completing the fianchetto, White should try to block our Bg7 before it appears by playing 7.d4. We still complete our fianchetto, continuing with kingside development.} 7...Bg7 8.Be3 {White defends d4 with the dark-squared bishop. We develop our knight to f6, trying to provoke e5 so we can later reroute it to d7 and prepare ...c5.} 8...Nf6 9.Bg2 {White now completes their fianchetto and can castle either long or short. It’s time for us to trade in the center with 9...Nxe4.} 9...Nxe4 10.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. We are forced to take back with our d-pawn, opening up the position.} 10...dxe4 11.Qxe4 {White has no choice but to capture back on e4 with the queen.} 11...Nd7 {We continue developing with 10...Nd7, reinforcing our structure. This is a typical setup that White aims for in the Two Knights. White has the bishop pair and more space, but our position is solid. We’ll castle and maneuver the knight to d5 via f6, followed by ...b5 to prevent c4 from kicking the knight away.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 8"]
