1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.g3 {Another option for White is 6.g3, aiming to fianchetto the light-squared bishop, played only about 5% of the time. Our pawn chain easily blocks that bishop, so there’s no need to worry. We respond with ...g6, going for our own fianchetto and placing more pawns on light squares.} 6...g6 7.Bg2 {White often plays 7.Bg2, completing the fianchetto, but this is actually a mistake. We also complete ours, developing our bishop onto the long, open diagonal.} 7...Bg7 8.O-O {White castles kingside.} 8...Ne7 {We continue our development with 8...Ne7, keeping the bishop active and preparing to castle next. Our other knight will head to d7.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 4"]
