1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.d3 {A common idea for White is to defend the center with 6.d3, a move we can expect to see about 20% of the time. Since White hasn’t occupied the center, we go for a very interesting idea, 6...g6, aiming to fianchetto our bishop on the long diagonal.} 6...g6 7.Bd2 {White develops the dark-squared bishop to d2, preparing to castle long. We also develop our dark-squared bishop, completing the fianchetto.} 7...Bg7 8.O-O-O {White castles queenside, bringing the king to safety.} 8...Nd7 {But our bishop is already eyeing the enemy king from a distance. We continue developing with 8...Nd7, followed by ...Ne7, getting ready for a sharp fight.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White can try to develop normally with 4.Be2, a move you’ll face about 16% of the time. We continue development with 4...e6 and ...Nf6, enjoying a solid light-square structure with our bishop safely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.O-O {White castles, bringing the king to safety. As mentioned, we continue with 5...Nf6, fighting for the center.} 5...Nf6 6.e5 {White advances with tempo using 6.e5, a variation that interestingly resembles the Advance Caro sidelines from Chapter 1. We retreat the knight to d7, keeping an eye on the central squares.} 6...Nfd7 7.d4 {White occupies the center with 7.d4, reinforcing e5. We continue developing with 7...Be7, getting ready to castle short.} 7...Be7 8.h3 {White plays 8.h3, asking our bishop to decide. We’re happy to exchange it for the knight since it removes one of White’s central defenders.} 8...Bxf3 9.Bxf3 {White recaptures with the bishop.} 9...c5 {It’s time for our typical ...c5 strike, just like in the Advance Caro. We give the c6 square to our knight, from where it will control d4 and e5, putting pressure on White’s center. We’ll also castle short, reaching a familiar Caro-Kann structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White can try to develop normally with 4.Be2, a move you’ll face about 16% of the time. We continue development with 4...e6 and ...Nf6, enjoying a solid light-square structure with our bishop safely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.O-O {White castles, bringing the king to safety. As mentioned, we continue with 5...Nf6, fighting for the center.} 5...Nf6 6.exd5 {Alternatively, White can take in the center with 6.exd5. This trade suits us, White gives up their central tension, and we simply recapture with the c-pawn.} 6...cxd5 7.d4 {White fights for the center with 7.d4.} 7...Nc6 {Surprisingly, the game transposes into an Exchange Caro-Kann, a structure we’ve already examined in previous studies. This version is very comfortable for us, as we have excellent development. We can continue with ...Be7 or ...d6, then castle short and look for queenside play with ...Rc8, ...a6–b5, or ...Na5–Nc4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: The Two Knights (Nf3+Nc3): Weapon 1: 3...Bg4 / Study 1: The Solid 3...Bg4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/4387ffe1-6615-4288-be06-8d3b876d4b29/studies/e081762d-6498-4aea-91b6-98be24ca3899/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will study the Two Knights, a solid system that White can play. We’ll focus on 3...Bg4, the solid main line, but with a slight twist of our own. You can find an alternative weapon against this system in the Bonus Lines course. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Caro-Kann: 1...c6.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, reinforcing the center. We play 2...d5, as we always do in the Caro-Kann, challenging the center and building our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.Nf3 {White develops the second knight to f3, entering the Two Knights Variation. It's played a quarter of the time. Our remedy against this solid setup is the main 3...Bg4, as mentioned in the introduction, bringing the bishop outside the pawn chain.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White’s best and most common reaction is to immediately question our bishop, a move played 42% of the time. We agree to trade and give up the bishop for the knight, since ...Bh5 could lead to dangerous g4 ideas. As a consequence, we’ll place all our pawns on light squares.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen, choosing not to damage their structure. As mentioned, we continue placing our pawns on light squares with 5...e6, compensating for the absence of our light-squared bishop.} 5...e6 6.d4 {Taking over the center with 6.d4 is White’s most natural and common move (played about 32% of the time). We counter this by developing with 6...Nf6, putting pressure on the center and keeping an eye on e4 and d5.} 6...Nf6 7.Bd3 {Another popular move by White is 7.Bd3, which comes with a gambit idea. We accept the gambit by capturing first on e4 with our pawn, planning to take on d4 next with the queen.} 7...dxe4 8.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. We’re now a pawn up after taking with 8...Qxd4, although White will gain time by attacking our queen.} 8...Qxd4 9.Be3 {White develops the bishop with tempo on our queen, a logical move. Instead of retreating the queen, we throw in a check with ...Bb4+, forcing the king to move and preventing White from castling. 10.c3 doesn’t work, as we would simply capture on d3.} 9...Bb4+ 10.Ke2 {White’s king escapes the check, and our queen is again under attack. We keep it active with 10...Qe5, aligning it on the same file as the enemy king.} 10...Qe5 11.Bf4 {White continues gaining tempos on our queen with 11.Bf4. We calmly slide it to d5, where it sits strongly. Our plan could be ...Nd7–e5.} 11...Qd5 12.Nxf6+ {White exchanges on f6 with check, doubling our f-pawns. We’re forced to recapture with 12...gxf6.} 12...gxf6 13.Be4 {White keeps gaining time with 13.Be4, attacking the queen again.} 13...Qc4+ {We reply with 13...Qc4+, giving a check. The game could end in a draw after 14.Bd3 Qd5 =, as the position repeats.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Fantasy (3. f3) Weapon 2: 3...e6 / Study 1: The Solid 3..e6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/fb737e0f-358a-4009-9015-bdf78e746d44/studies/9c3b65f8-c455-47b4-9074-5079a6833072/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 7, where we’ll study the Fantasy Variation with 3.f3, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro-Kann. Our way to respond will be with 3...e6, a solid approach. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. The whole idea behind 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so we play it.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White aims to maintain their central pawn chain with 3.f3, and we now officially enter the Fantasy Variation. It's played 4% of the time, but was still seen in more than 3.7 million games. As mentioned in the introduction, we play 3...e6, building a Caro-Kann pyramid and reinforcing control of the d5-square.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White’s most common option, played 42% of the time, is to develop and support the e4-pawn. We respond with the active ...Qb6, targeting d4 and b2, making it difficult for White to develop smoothly.} 4...Qb6 5.e5 {White plays 5.e5 in about 12% of games, gaining space in the center. When White releases the tension like this, we can immediately strike back with 5...c5, and that’s exactly what we do next.} 5...c5 6.Nge2 {White develops and tries to hold on to the d4-pawn. After 6.Nge2, we develop our knight behind the c-pawn, adding more pressure to d4. Of course, if 6.dxc5, then 6...Bxc5 would expose White’s position along the g1–a7 diagonal.} 6...Nc6 7.Be3 {White continues developing and defending d4, this time with 7.Be3. The problem with this move is 7...Nh6, immediately going after the dark-squared bishop with our knight and increasing the pressure.} 7...Nh6 8.dxc5 {White may now decide it’s time to release the tension by capturing on c5. However, that only helps us speed up development, as we can recapture on c5 with our bishop.} 8...Bxc5 9.Bxc5 {White trades pieces with 9.Bxc5. We recapture with the queen, and it becomes clear that the e5-pawn is hard to defend.} 9...Qxc5 10.f4 {White tries to defend e5 with 10.f4.} 10...Ng4 {That defense isn’t ideal and allows us to create even bigger threats with 10...Ng4, with both the queen and the knight eyeing f2. Black is winning here as White has no way to defend against the threat.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Fantasy (3. f3) Weapon 2: 3...e6 / Study 1: The Solid 3..e6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/fb737e0f-358a-4009-9015-bdf78e746d44/studies/9c3b65f8-c455-47b4-9074-5079a6833072/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 7, where we’ll study the Fantasy Variation with 3.f3, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro-Kann. Our way to respond will be with 3...e6, a solid approach. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. The whole idea behind 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so we play it.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White aims to maintain their central pawn chain with 3.f3, and we now officially enter the Fantasy Variation. It's played 4% of the time, but was still seen in more than 3.7 million games. As mentioned in the introduction, we play 3...e6, building a Caro-Kann pyramid and reinforcing control of the d5-square.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White’s most common option, played 42% of the time, is to develop and support the e4-pawn. We respond with the active ...Qb6, targeting d4 and b2, making it difficult for White to develop smoothly.} 4...Qb6 5.Nge2 {White calmly develops with 5.Nge2, the most popular move, played about 15% of the time, but the pieces are awkwardly placed. We respond with 5...Nf6, a move we sometimes delay to avoid e5, but here it’s perfectly timed since White can’t follow up with f4–Nf3.} 5...Nf6 6.e5 {White pushes in the center, gaining a tempo on our knight. We retreat to d7, preparing to counter with ...c5.} 6...Nfd7 7.f4 {White reinforces the center with 7.f4. We have solid support for the c5 push, so we play it, challenging the d4-pawn.} 7...c5 8.Na4 {White plays 8.Na4, trying to chase our queen away. We slide our queen to c6, attacking the knight.} 8...Qc6 9.Nxc5 {White escapes danger by capturing on c5 with the knight. This only helps us develop, as we trade knights with 9...Nxc5.} 9...Nxc5 10.dxc5 {White recaptures with the d-pawn. We develop our bishop by recapturing on c5, placing it on a powerful diagonal.} 10...Bxc5 11.Nd4 {White tries to block the diagonal with 11.Nd4, which also frees their light-squared bishop for development. We move our queen to b6, adding pressure to d4.} 11...Qb6 12.c3 {White defends the knight with 12.c3, reinforcing it with the pawn. We continue developing while adding pressure on d4, bringing the knight to c6.} 12...Nc6 13.Bb5 {White pins our knight with 13.Bb5 to save theirs. We castle, getting out of the pin and completing development.} 13...O-O 14.Bxc6 {White exchanges their bishop for our knight, as otherwise we’d capture on d4. We recapture with the b-pawn, opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 14...bxc6 15.b4 {White tries to push us back with 15.b4. We retreat our bishop to e7, calmly preparing to play ...c5.} 15...Be7 16.Be3 {White develops their bishop to e3, x-raying our queen. We’re just in time to play 16...c5, blocking the bishop’s view and breaking open the center.} 16...c5 17.Nb3 {White retreats the knight to b3, trying to use the pin to win a pawn. We push our d-pawn, attacking the bishop, a powerful counterstrike supported by a tactical idea.} 17...d4 18.bxc5 {White captures on c5, winning the pawn.} 18...Qc6 {But we slide our queen to c6, creating a strong double attack that hits both the bishop and the g2-pawn. Our dark-squared bishop can head to h4 and the light-squared one to a6, controlling key diagonals. With open files and active pieces, we reach a dominant position to conclude the variation.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Fantasy (3. f3) Weapon 2: 3...e6 / Study 1: The Solid 3..e6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/fb737e0f-358a-4009-9015-bdf78e746d44/studies/9c3b65f8-c455-47b4-9074-5079a6833072/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 7, where we’ll study the Fantasy Variation with 3.f3, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro-Kann. Our way to respond will be with 3...e6, a solid approach. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. The whole idea behind 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so we play it.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White aims to maintain their central pawn chain with 3.f3, and we now officially enter the Fantasy Variation. It's played 4% of the time, but was still seen in more than 3.7 million games. As mentioned in the introduction, we play 3...e6, building a Caro-Kann pyramid and reinforcing control of the d5-square.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White’s most common option, played 42% of the time, is to develop and support the e4-pawn. We respond with the active ...Qb6, targeting d4 and b2, making it difficult for White to develop smoothly.} 4...Qb6 5.a3 {Playing 5.a3 has become the main line, allowing White to develop their dark-squared bishop. We simply continue development with 5...Nd7, supporting the key ...e5 and ...c5 breaks.} 5...Nd7 6.Be3 {White’s whole plan was to play 6.Be3, and now that the b2-pawn is poisoned, they’ve accomplished it. We cannot take on b2 because of 6.Na4. Instead, we continue developing with 6...Ngf6, adding pressure to the center.} 6...Ngf6 7.Bd3 {Safeguarding the center with 7.Bd3 is one of White’s ways to handle the position. We strike with 7...c5, one of the key thematic ideas of the whole ...Qb6 variation. This break not only activates our pieces but also exploits the weakness created by 3.f3.} 7...c5 8.Na4 {With 8.Na4, White goes for a forcing line against our pawn break. We calmly retreat the queen to c7, keeping everything protected.} 8...Qc7 9.dxc5 {White captures on c5 with the pawn. We recapture with the knight, maintaining material balance.} 9...Nxc5 10.Nxc5 {White continues the exchanges, taking again on c5 with the knight.} 10...Bxc5 {We recapture with the bishop, leaving ourselves one move away from castling, with good central control and an easy position to play.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Fantasy (3. f3) Weapon 2: 3...e6 / Study 1: The Solid 3..e6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/fb737e0f-358a-4009-9015-bdf78e746d44/studies/9c3b65f8-c455-47b4-9074-5079a6833072/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 7, where we’ll study the Fantasy Variation with 3.f3, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro-Kann. Our way to respond will be with 3...e6, a solid approach. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. The whole idea behind 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so we play it.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White aims to maintain their central pawn chain with 3.f3, and we now officially enter the Fantasy Variation. It's played 4% of the time, but was still seen in more than 3.7 million games. As mentioned in the introduction, we play 3...e6, building a Caro-Kann pyramid and reinforcing control of the d5-square.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White’s most common option, played 42% of the time, is to develop and support the e4-pawn. We respond with the active ...Qb6, targeting d4 and b2, making it difficult for White to develop smoothly.} 4...Qb6 5.a3 {Playing 5.a3 has become the main line, allowing White to develop their dark-squared bishop. We simply continue development with 5...Nd7, supporting the key ...e5 and ...c5 breaks.} 5...Nd7 6.Be3 {White’s whole plan was to play 6.Be3, and now that the b2-pawn is poisoned, they’ve accomplished it. We cannot take on b2 because of 6.Na4. Instead, we continue developing with 6...Ngf6, adding pressure to the center.} 6...Ngf6 7.Rb1 {White can also directly protect the b2-pawn with 7.Rb1. It’s time for us to strike back in the center with 7...dxe4.} 7...dxe4 8.fxe4 {White recaptures with the f-pawn, a logical move to keep the central pawns connected. We push in the center with 8...e5, blocking White's pawns. White cannot capture since the bishop on e3 would be hanging. We’ll continue with ...Qc7, ...Bd6, and ...O-O, and eventually take on d4 at the right moment to liberate our pieces.} 8...e5 9.Nf3 {White develops the other knight, attacking e5 and defending d4. We slide our queen back to c7, defending the e5-pawn and stepping out of the bishop’s scope.} 9...Qc7 10.Be2 {White gets ready to castle.} 10...Bd6 {We do the same with 10...Bd6, reinforcing the e5-pawn and creating a strong dark-square formation. We'll castle next with a balanced position.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Fantasy (3. f3) Weapon 2: 3...e6 / Study 1: The Solid 3..e6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/fb737e0f-358a-4009-9015-bdf78e746d44/studies/9c3b65f8-c455-47b4-9074-5079a6833072/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 7, where we’ll study the Fantasy Variation with 3.f3, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro-Kann. Our way to respond will be with 3...e6, a solid approach. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. The whole idea behind 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so we play it.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White aims to maintain their central pawn chain with 3.f3, and we now officially enter the Fantasy Variation. It's played 4% of the time, but was still seen in more than 3.7 million games. As mentioned in the introduction, we play 3...e6, building a Caro-Kann pyramid and reinforcing control of the d5-square.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White’s most common option, played 42% of the time, is to develop and support the e4-pawn. We respond with the active ...Qb6, targeting d4 and b2, making it difficult for White to develop smoothly.} 4...Qb6 5.a3 {Playing 5.a3 has become the main line, allowing White to develop their dark-squared bishop. We simply continue development with 5...Nd7, supporting the key ...e5 and ...c5 breaks.} 5...Nd7 6.Be3 {White’s whole plan was to play 6.Be3, and now that the b2-pawn is poisoned, they’ve accomplished it. We cannot take on b2 because of 6.Na4. Instead, we continue developing with 6...Ngf6, adding pressure to the center.} 6...Ngf6 7.e5 {White has fallen for this trick in a surprising number of games! Pushing e5 is a clear blunder.} 7...Nxe5 {That’s just a free pawn! We capture on e5 with our knight. The d4 pawn is pinned because the bishop behind it is hanging!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Fantasy (3. f3) Weapon 2: 3...e6 / Study 1: The Solid 3..e6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/fb737e0f-358a-4009-9015-bdf78e746d44/studies/9c3b65f8-c455-47b4-9074-5079a6833072/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 7, where we’ll study the Fantasy Variation with 3.f3, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro-Kann. Our way to respond will be with 3...e6, a solid approach. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. The whole idea behind 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so we play it.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White aims to maintain their central pawn chain with 3.f3, and we now officially enter the Fantasy Variation. It's played 4% of the time, but was still seen in more than 3.7 million games. As mentioned in the introduction, we play 3...e6, building a Caro-Kann pyramid and reinforcing control of the d5-square.} 3...e6 4.e5 {White takes space in the center with 4.e5, a very common pawn push online, played about 16% of the time. White’s pawns are overextended, and our counterplay becomes clear with 4...c5, putting immediate pressure on d4.} 4...c5 5.c3 {White reinforces the center with 5.c3, building a defensive pawn chain. We develop behind the c-pawn with 5...Nc6, adding more pressure to d4.} 5...Nc6 6.f4 {White’s next move defends e5 and prepares the f3-square for the knight. It’s time for our common queen move, 6...Qb6, increasing the pressure on d4 and preventing the dark-squared bishop from developing.} 6...Qb6 7.Nf3 {White continues to develop and defend the center. We respond with 7...Nh6, a useful maneuver to route the knight toward f5, adding another attacker on d4.} 7...Nh6 8.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop, keeping an eye on the f5 square. Now it’s time for us to capture on d4.} 8...cxd4 9.cxd4 {White recaptures with the pawn. We continue with 9...Bd7, a calm-looking move that threatens to capture on d4, since it eliminates any Bb5+ ideas.} 9...Bd7 10.Bc2 {White’s bishop retreats, and now the d4-pawn is reinforced. We go after White’s strong bishop with 10...Nb4.} 10...Nb4 11.Nc3 {White develops the other knight. We capture on c2, gaining the bishop pair advantage.} 11...Nxc2+ 12.Qxc2 {White recaptures with the queen. Now that the light-squared bishop is gone, we occupy the f5-square with our knight, adding another attacker to d4.} 12...Nf5 13.Qd3 {White defends the pawn with the queen.} 13...h5 {We play a nice idea, preventing any g4-pushes that would kick our knight away from f5. Next, we’ll finish development with ...Be7 and ...O-O, followed by ...Rc8, giving us strong queenside play.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Fantasy (3. f3) Weapon 2: 3...e6 / Study 1: The Solid 3..e6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/fb737e0f-358a-4009-9015-bdf78e746d44/studies/9c3b65f8-c455-47b4-9074-5079a6833072/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 7, where we’ll study the Fantasy Variation with 3.f3, a rare but tricky and aggressive system against the Caro-Kann. Our way to respond will be with 3...e6, a solid approach. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center with 2.d4. The whole idea behind 1...c6 is to prepare 2...d5, so we play it.} 2...d5 3.f3 {White aims to maintain their central pawn chain with 3.f3, and we now officially enter the Fantasy Variation. It's played 4% of the time, but was still seen in more than 3.7 million games. As mentioned in the introduction, we play 3...e6, building a Caro-Kann pyramid and reinforcing control of the d5-square.} 3...e6 4.Be3 {Defending the d4-pawn with the dark-squared bishop, 4.Be3, is an option White chooses about 6% of the time. We respond with 4...Qb6, a nice idea that highlights the drawback of leaving b2 undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {White’s most common reaction is to push the b-pawn with 5.b3, preventing the capture. We immediately strike at the center with 5...c5.} 5...c5 6.c3 {White tries to keep the center intact with 6.c3. We continue with natural development, 6...Nc6, increasing central pressure.} 6...Nc6 7.Bd3 {White keeps reinforcing the center with their pieces, 7.Bd3. Our strategy is clear: develop and attack White’s pawns. And there’s even a nice trap hidden in the position.} 7...Nf6 8.e5 {White advances in the center with 8.e5, gaining a tempo on our knight, but this move falls right into our trap. We begin by capturing on d4, attacking the bishop with our pawn.} 8...cxd4 9.cxd4 {White is forced to recapture on d4.} 9...Nxe5 {And now comes the blow, 9.Nxe5!, capturing the key e5 pawn thanks to the pin. We leave the opening a pawn up and with an excellent position.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 1: The Pesky Pseudo-Panov / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/ec833913-e13c-4bc8-a24b-ce6202c2256c/lines"]

1.e4 {You’ve made it to the final chapter of the Caro-Kann, where we’ll cover all the variations that weren’t included in the earlier chapters. The common theme throughout these upcoming studies is that White’s lines are rare, unusual, or sometimes even bizarre! We start with the Pseudo-Panov, a respectable cousin of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack. White begins, as always, with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann!} 1...c6 2.c4 {White plays 2.c4, an uncommon move known as the Pseudo-Panov, 2% of the time. It can transpose into the real Panov, but usually White will throw in a disruptive check. Our response is always the same: we push 2...d5, claiming the center.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {If White captures with the e-pawn, it allows us to play an interesting gambit. We can expect 3.exd5 in about a quarter of games. Instead of recapturing on d5, we focus on rapid development with 3...Nf6!} 3...Nf6 4.dxc6 {White shouldn’t accept the gambit, it was better to play 4.d4 or Nc3. However, these moves transpose into lines we’ve already studied, so let’s see why this gambit is so strong. We continue by recapturing and developing at the same time with 4...Nxc6.} 4...Nxc6 5.Nf3 {White tries to catch up by developing with 5.Nf3, but we can quickly generate threats. We strike in the center with 5...e5, opening the f8-bishop and threatening e4.} 5...e5 6.d3 {White takes control of the e4 square with 6.d3, preparing to develop the c1-bishop. We’re better developed, so we go for direct confrontation with 6...e4!} 6...e4 7.dxe4 {White might think that trading queens after 7.dxe4 brings some relief. But we happily exchange queens, leaving White’s king misplaced in the center.} 7...Qxd1+ 8.Kxd1 {White recaptures with the king.} 8...Nxe4 {We now capture on e4, gaining great activity and multiple threats across the board. Our bishops will come to c5 and f5 and our knight will jump to b4. We can castle long. In contrast, the white king is far from safe.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 1: The Pesky Pseudo-Panov / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/ec833913-e13c-4bc8-a24b-ce6202c2256c/lines"]

1.e4 {You’ve made it to the final chapter of the Caro-Kann, where we’ll cover all the variations that weren’t included in the earlier chapters. The common theme throughout these upcoming studies is that White’s lines are rare, unusual, or sometimes even bizarre! We start with the Pseudo-Panov, a respectable cousin of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack. White begins, as always, with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann!} 1...c6 2.c4 {White plays 2.c4, an uncommon move known as the Pseudo-Panov, 2% of the time. It can transpose into the real Panov, but usually White will throw in a disruptive check. Our response is always the same: we push 2...d5, claiming the center.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {White’s most common reply is to capture on d5, seen in about 67% of games. We simply recapture the pawn, restoring central balance.} 3...cxd5 4.exd5 {White continues trading on d5, which happens in 76% of games. We don’t rush to recapture with the queen, since it would be chased by Nc3. Instead, we play 4...Nf6 to target the pawn, a more efficient approach.} 4...Nf6 5.Qa4+ {The check with the queen is the signature move of the Pseudo-Panov, aiming to disrupt our development. It’s only played about 6% of the time. We calmly block with 5...Nbd7, and later we’ll look to gain a tempo on the queen with ...Nb6.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, protecting d5. We prepare to castle by playing 6...g6, intending to fianchetto our bishop.} 6...g6 7.Nf3 {White continues development with 7.Nf3. We do the same, completing our fianchetto.} 7...Bg7 8.Bc4 {White develops further, defending d5 and getting one move away from castling. We play 8...a6, even though ...b5 isn’t yet possible because of the pin on the a-file, but it’s a useful idea for the near future.} 8...a6 9.d3 {White builds an odd-looking vertical formation on the light squares. This pawn move supports the c4-bishop against a later ...Nb6. We castle kingside, and now ...b5 becomes a real threat, since we can follow up with ...Nb6, defending our loose a8-rook.} 9...O-O 10.Qa3 {White’s best move is to step aside and prevent the ...b5 push.} 10...b6 {We calmly continue with 10...b6 instead, nice and slow. Our next steps are simple: to play ...Bb7, then ...b5 and ...Nb6. The d5-pawn won’t survive for long, and afterwards we’ll enjoy excellent development and harmony, while White will be left with yet another weak d-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 1: The Pesky Pseudo-Panov / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/ec833913-e13c-4bc8-a24b-ce6202c2256c/lines"]

1.e4 {You’ve made it to the final chapter of the Caro-Kann, where we’ll cover all the variations that weren’t included in the earlier chapters. The common theme throughout these upcoming studies is that White’s lines are rare, unusual, or sometimes even bizarre! We start with the Pseudo-Panov, a respectable cousin of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack. White begins, as always, with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann!} 1...c6 2.c4 {White plays 2.c4, an uncommon move known as the Pseudo-Panov, 2% of the time. It can transpose into the real Panov, but usually White will throw in a disruptive check. Our response is always the same: we push 2...d5, claiming the center.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {White’s most common reply is to capture on d5, seen in about 67% of games. We simply recapture the pawn, restoring central balance.} 3...cxd5 4.exd5 {White continues trading on d5, which happens in 76% of games. We don’t rush to recapture with the queen, since it would be chased by Nc3. Instead, we play 4...Nf6 to target the pawn, a more efficient approach.} 4...Nf6 5.Bb5+ {White can try to disrupt our development by checking on b5 with the light-squared bishop, a move seen about 10% of the time. We block the check with the knight. This pin is only a mild inconvenience, as we’ll soon play ...a6.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White defends d5 by developing the knight to c3. We continue with 6...a6, not only chasing the b5-bishop, but also preparing ...b5 and ...Bb7.} 6...a6 7.Qa4 {White continues with 7.Qa4, pinning the a6 pawn and desperately trying to keep the pin alive.} 7...Rb8 {We slide out of it with 7...Rb8, an excellent move to unpin. With ...b5 coming soon, the d5 pawn is far too weak to survive for long.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 1: The Pesky Pseudo-Panov / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/ec833913-e13c-4bc8-a24b-ce6202c2256c/lines"]

1.e4 {You’ve made it to the final chapter of the Caro-Kann, where we’ll cover all the variations that weren’t included in the earlier chapters. The common theme throughout these upcoming studies is that White’s lines are rare, unusual, or sometimes even bizarre! We start with the Pseudo-Panov, a respectable cousin of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack. White begins, as always, with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann!} 1...c6 2.c4 {White plays 2.c4, an uncommon move known as the Pseudo-Panov, 2% of the time. It can transpose into the real Panov, but usually White will throw in a disruptive check. Our response is always the same: we push 2...d5, claiming the center.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {White’s most common reply is to capture on d5, seen in about 67% of games. We simply recapture the pawn, restoring central balance.} 3...cxd5 4.e5 {Pushing the e-pawn leads to a strange version of the Advance Caro-Kann, but it’s played about 15% of the time. We respond with 4...Nc6, developing actively and putting pressure on the center.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {White defends the attacked e-pawn by expanding in the center with 5.d4. We take the opportunity to bring out our bishop before playing ...e6, making sure it doesn’t get blocked in.} 5...Bf5 6.Nc3 {White develops logically with 6.Nc3. We continue with our usual preparatory move 6...e6, getting ready to complete our kingside development.} 6...e6 7.Nf3 {White continues developing with 7.Nf3.} 7...Bg4 {We respond with the strong 7...Bg4, losing a tempo but creating a powerful pin. The plan is simple: follow up with ...Ne7–f5, adding significant pressure on the d4-pawn and seizing the initiative.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 1: The Pesky Pseudo-Panov / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/ec833913-e13c-4bc8-a24b-ce6202c2256c/lines"]

1.e4 {You’ve made it to the final chapter of the Caro-Kann, where we’ll cover all the variations that weren’t included in the earlier chapters. The common theme throughout these upcoming studies is that White’s lines are rare, unusual, or sometimes even bizarre! We start with the Pseudo-Panov, a respectable cousin of the Panov–Botvinnik Attack. White begins, as always, with 1.e4, and we reply with the Caro-Kann!} 1...c6 2.c4 {White plays 2.c4, an uncommon move known as the Pseudo-Panov, 2% of the time. It can transpose into the real Panov, but usually White will throw in a disruptive check. Our response is always the same: we push 2...d5, claiming the center.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {White’s most common reply is to capture on d5, seen in about 67% of games. We simply recapture the pawn, restoring central balance.} 3...cxd5 4.exd5 {White continues trading on d5, which happens in 76% of games. We don’t rush to recapture with the queen, since it would be chased by Nc3. Instead, we play 4...Nf6 to target the pawn, a more efficient approach.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {5.Nc3 often transposes into the Panov, and it's played about 64% of the time. However, it’s the most common move since many players blitz it out automatically to defend the pawn. We immediately capture on d5 with our knight.} 5...Nxd5 6.Nf3 {White continues with natural development, 6.Nf3. Instead, 6.Bc4 is met with ...Nb6, and 6.d4 can be answered with ...g6, transposing directly into the Panov.} 6...g6 {We end the variation by preparing our fianchetto and castling, just like in the Panov!} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 2: The Failed Kings Indian (2. d3) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/27f29090-9d95-46a3-9902-7066d408ca4a/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we’ll analyze the timid 2.d3. I like to call it the “Failed King’s Indian” because White tries to set up a King’s Indian Attack structure, but it works very poorly against the Caro, where we can easily dominate the center. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Gotham Caro.} 1...c6 2.d3 {The move 2.d3 is our object of study, played 3% of the time, but there are die-hard King’s Indian fans who still play it! However, it’s simply ineffective against the Caro-Kann. As always, our solution is to first place one pawn in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nd2 {White continues with the usual King’s Indian Attack routine, playing 3.Nd2. We respond with 3...e5, establishing two pawns in the center!} 3...e5 4.Ngf3 {White develops the kingside knight to f3, attacking our e5-pawn. We defend it naturally by developing our bishop to d6.} 4...Bd6 5.d4 {Playing 5.d4 is a slightly more enterprising idea. It may look threatening, but it’s easy to neutralize. It’s played about 14% of the time. Our solution is to capture on d4, reducing the central tension.} 5...exd4 6.exd5 {White also trades by capturing on d5. We simply recapture with our c-pawn.} 6...cxd5 7.Nxd4 {White recaptures on d4 with the knight. Now the center is simplified, making development easier. We continue developing with ...Nc6, gaining a tempo on the knight on d4.} 7...Nc6 8.N2f3 {White defends d4 with the other knight.} 8...Nf6 {We keep developing with 8...Nf6, following our usual plan. Our ideas are simple, castle short and play ...Bg4, pinning the knight to the queen and completing development.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 2: The Failed Kings Indian (2. d3) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/27f29090-9d95-46a3-9902-7066d408ca4a/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we’ll analyze the timid 2.d3. I like to call it the “Failed King’s Indian” because White tries to set up a King’s Indian Attack structure, but it works very poorly against the Caro, where we can easily dominate the center. As always, White starts with 1.e4, and we respond with the Gotham Caro.} 1...c6 2.d3 {The move 2.d3 is our object of study, played 3% of the time, but there are die-hard King’s Indian fans who still play it! However, it’s simply ineffective against the Caro-Kann. As always, our solution is to first place one pawn in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nd2 {White continues with the usual King’s Indian Attack routine, playing 3.Nd2. We respond with 3...e5, establishing two pawns in the center!} 3...e5 4.Ngf3 {White develops the kingside knight to f3, attacking our e5-pawn. We defend it naturally by developing our bishop to d6.} 4...Bd6 5.g3 {White goes for the standard King’s Indian fianchetto, which is played around 60% of the time in this position. We continue developing smoothly, preparing to castle.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg2 {White completes the fianchetto. We castle short, bringing our king to safety.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White castles as well, still following the typical King’s Indian setup. We slide our rook to the e-file, reinforcing our center.} 7...Re8 8.c3 {White’s next move, 8.c3, is not the only option but a reasonable one, as it controls key squares and prepares b4.} 8...a5 {We acknowledge this idea and stop b4 with 8...a5 while also initiating our queenside play. Next, we can continue developing with ...Nd7, ...b6 and ...Ba6, activating all of our queenside pieces and enjoying a comfortable game.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 3: The Goldman Caro (2. Nc3 3. Qf3!?) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/c2337eb6-ea0a-4260-b790-bc41a2c3718c/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we analyze some interesting early queen moves, including GM Naroditsky’s favorite, the Goldman Caro. White starts by taking the center, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the b-knight to control the center. We establish the Caro structure with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Qe2 {3.Qe2 is a bizarre move, aimed at freeing the d1-square for the knight! It’s played in only about 1% of games, though that’s still over 70,000 in databases! Our remedy is simple: push in the center with 3...d4.} 3...d4 4.Nd1 {White retreats the attacked knight to d1, planning f4 followed by Nd1–Nf2 to build a kingside attack. We continue taking space in the center with 4...e5.} 4...e5 5.g3 {5.g3 appears on the board, preparing f4. We keep taking over the center with 5...c5.} 5...c5 6.d3 {White plays 6.d3, opening the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop, which now supports the f4 push. We continue developing with 6...Nc6.} 6...Nc6 7.Bg2 {White fianchettos the light-squared bishop. We keep developing as well with 7...Bd6.} 7...Bd6 8.f4 {It’s now time for White to play 8.f4, starting kingside play. We capture on f4, an interesting idea to create a long-term target.} 8...exf4 9.gxf4 {White recaptures and builds a strong pawn center.} 9...f6 {We conclude the variation with 9...f6, restricting White’s pawns and preparing ...Nge7 and ...Qc7 for a complex game. Depending on the circumstances, we can castle short or long.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 3: The Goldman Caro (2. Nc3 3. Qf3!?) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/c2337eb6-ea0a-4260-b790-bc41a2c3718c/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we analyze some interesting early queen moves, including GM Naroditsky’s favorite, the Goldman Caro. White starts by taking the center, and we reply with 1...c6, the Caro-Kann.} 1...c6 2.Nc3 {White develops the b-knight to control the center. We establish the Caro structure with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Qf3 {The Goldman Caro appears on the board with 3.Qf3, a move championed by GM Daniel Naroditsky in many games and speedruns! However, its overall popularity in databases is just about 2%. We respond in the simplest way, by capturing on e4.} 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight. We play 4...Nd7, not only preparing ...Nf6, but also preventing Bc4 due to ...Ne5.} 4...Nd7 5.d4 {White wisely takes the center and prepares Bc4. We respond with 5...Ndf6, a peculiar-looking move that uses the d7-knight instead of the g8-knight, gaining a tempo against d4.} 5...Ndf6 6.c3 {White defends the d-pawn with 6.c3. We first trade on e4, since our knights are stepping on each other’s toes.} 6...Nxe4 7.Qxe4 {The queen recaptures the knight. We develop our remaining knight to f6, doing so with tempo.} 7...Nf6 8.Qc2 {The queen retreats to c2, moving to safety.} 8...Bg4 {We finish the variation with 8...Bg4, planning to reroute our bishop to the tempting h7–b1 diagonal with tempo.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 4: Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them (2. b3, 2. Bc4, 2.f4, etc) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/90246d3d-75eb-451f-b68d-696abfc79af4/lines"]

1.e4 {Let’s wrap things up by looking at all the odd White tries that don’t fit anywhere else. Even the wildest Caro deviations begin with 1.e4. As always, we reply with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.f4 {White tries 2.f4, a relatively popular move but nothing to fear: it resembles some ideas we saw back in Chapter 1. It was played in about 4% of games and nearly 7.5 million times. As always we respond with 2...d5, establishing our Caro structure.} 2...d5 3.e5 {After 3.e5, we get a strange version of the Advance Caro. We'll counter with 3...c5, taking control of the d4-square and preparing to develop our knight to c6.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {White develops naturally with 4.Nf3, reinforcing the center. We continue our plan with 4...Nc6, following up on our previous move.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White might try to pin our knight with the light-squared bishop, but it doesn’t really achieve much. We calmly play 5...Qb6, attacking b2 while defending c6.} 5...Qb6 6.Bxc6+ {White decides to trade on c6 with check. We recapture with the queen, keeping our pawn structure healthy.} 6...Qxc6 7.O-O {White castles kingside, bringing the king to safety.} 7...Nh6 {We conclude with 7...Nh6, a flexible developing move. Our light-squared bishop will soon head to g4 and our knight to f5, taking over the light squares that White weakened with the f4- and e5-pushes.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 4: Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them (2. b3, 2. Bc4, 2.f4, etc) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/90246d3d-75eb-451f-b68d-696abfc79af4/lines"]

1.e4 {Let’s wrap things up by looking at all the odd White tries that don’t fit anywhere else. Even the wildest Caro deviations begin with 1.e4. As always, we reply with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.Bc4 {Developing the bishop to c4 can lead to the infamous Hillbilly Attack. It’s seen in about 7% of games. We immediately seize a free tempo with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Bb3 {White retreats the bishop to b3, basically going for an advanced version of the Scholar’s Mate. Instead of worrying, we simply capture the center pawn. If White had taken on d5, we’d get an easy tempo on the bishop and develop smoothly with ...Nc6 or ...Nf6.} 3...dxe4 4.Qh5 {White brings the queen out to h5, openly threatening a devastating check on f7. We calmly respond with 4...e6, blocking the bishop.} 4...e6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight, adding pressure to our e4 pawn. We meet it with 4...Nf6, defending e4 and gaining a tempo on the queen.} 5...Nf6 6.Qh4 {White slides the queen back to safety while still eyeing e4. We develop our other knight to d7, quietly setting up a tactical fork idea.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nxe4 {White finally recovers the pawn by capturing on e4 with the knight. We’re happy to trade and recapture.} 7...Nxe4 8.Qxe4 {The queen is forced to recapture on e4.} 8...Nc5 {Our plan all along was the fork on c5, and now it lands, winning the bishop pair. After that, we’ll continue with ...Be7 and castle. Later, we'll aim for ...b6, ...Bb7, and ...c5 to free our light-squared bishop completely.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 4: Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them (2. b3, 2. Bc4, 2.f4, etc) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/90246d3d-75eb-451f-b68d-696abfc79af4/lines"]

1.e4 {Let’s wrap things up by looking at all the odd White tries that don’t fit anywhere else. Even the wildest Caro deviations begin with 1.e4. As always, we reply with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {At first, this looks perfectly normal for White, so why is it in the sidelines chapter? We continue as always with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Bd3 {White can also try the sideline with 3.Bd3, defending e4 with the bishop. It’s played in only about 1% of games. We can simply capture on e4 without any risk.} 3...dxe4 4.Bxe4 {White recaptures with the bishop, keeping a solid center. We develop our knight to f6, gaining a useful tempo by attacking that same bishop.} 4...Nf6 5.Bf3 {White retreats to f3, though that square would be much better for a knight. We continue smoothly with 5...Bf5, developing actively.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne2 {White plays Ne2, planning Ng3 and short castling. After 6...e6, we’ve already reached a comfortable and classical Caro–Kann setup.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles, bringing the king to safety. We place our dark-squared bishop on the long diagonal, where it exerts pressure.} 7...Bd6 8.c4 {White plays 8.c4, a strong move to control the d5-square. We don’t need to react, and we can simply castle and complete our development.} 8...O-O 9.Nbc3 {White develops the other knight to c3, strengthening the center. We finish development with 9...Nbd7, planning ...h6 to reposition the bishop. We intend to follow up with ...Qc7 and ...Re8 for a perfect setup.} 9...Nbd7 10.Ng3 {If White plays 10.Ng3, going after our bishop, there’s no reason to worry.} 10...Bg6 {We can get the bishop out of danger and continue with our plan, developing the queen and the rook. The queenside rook can join on d8 or c8, and we have an equal but promising position on our hands.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 4: Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them (2. b3, 2. Bc4, 2.f4, etc) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/90246d3d-75eb-451f-b68d-696abfc79af4/lines"]

1.e4 {Let’s wrap things up by looking at all the odd White tries that don’t fit anywhere else. Even the wildest Caro deviations begin with 1.e4. As always, we reply with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.b3 {White goes for a queenside fianchetto against the Caro, an unusual and rare idea. It’s played in less than 1% of games (around 500,000). We stick to our usual plan with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Bb2 {White completes the fianchetto and gambits the e4-pawn to open the long diagonal for the bishop. Free pawns are hard to refuse, so we gladly take it.} 3...dxe4 4.Nc3 {White plays 4.Nc3, attacking e4 and hinting at long castling later. We combine development with defense by playing 4...Nf6, solidifying our position.} 4...Nf6 5.Nge2 {Still chasing the e4-pawn, White continues with 5.Nge2. We develop our bishop to f5, reinforcing the pawn.} 5...Bf5 6.Ng3 {White completes the knight maneuver to g3, adding more pressure to e4 and threatening our bishop. We calmly defend with 6...e6, securing f5 and preparing to finish kingside development.} 6...e6 7.Qe2 {White now brings the queen into play, increasing the pressure while preparing O-O-O. We stay patient and develop with 7...Nbd7.} 7...Nbd7 8.Ngxe4 {White finally regains the pawn, restoring material balance. We keep developing harmoniously with ...Be7, preparing to castle short and complete our setup.} 8...Be7 9.O-O-O {White castles long, but is it any good?} 9...a5 {We finish the variation with a classic anti-fianchetto strategy! The pawn on b3 becomes our main attacking hook. From here, we’ll launch a typical queenside storm against the White king.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 4: Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them (2. b3, 2. Bc4, 2.f4, etc) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/90246d3d-75eb-451f-b68d-696abfc79af4/lines"]

1.e4 {Let’s wrap things up by looking at all the odd White tries that don’t fit anywhere else. Even the wildest Caro deviations begin with 1.e4. As always, we reply with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.d4 {At first, this looks perfectly normal for White, so why is it in the sidelines chapter? We continue as always with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {Still normal with 3.Nc3, developing toward the center. As in previous chapters, our solution is to exchange in the center and take control.} 3...dxe4 4.f3 {Now White surprises us with the gambit 4.f3!, a rare but playable try. It’s only seen in around 6% of games, but that's still over 780,000 in total! We can safely accept it.} 4...exf3 5.Nxf3 {White’s idea is that the pawn sacrifice will accelerate development and open lines for attack. We stay calm and develop with 5...Nf6.} 5...Nf6 6.Bc4 {White develops their light-squared bishop to an active diagonal, eyeing f7. We mirror with ...Bf5, and must remember: don’t blunder with 6...Bg4 because 7.Bxf7+ followed by Ne5+ wins immediately.} 6...Bf5 7.O-O {White castles, showing the attacking potential behind the gambit. We continue with the simple and strong 7...e6, preparing development and blocking the bishop on c4.} 7...e6 8.Ne5 {White jumps in with Ne5, a dangerous-looking move aiming at f5 and f7. We calmly retreat our bishop to g6, covering f7 and sidestepping any Nxf7 or Rxf5 tactics.} 8...Bg6 9.Bg5 {White develops actively again with 9.Bg5, pinning our knight. We defend it with our other knight, keeping everything coordinated and our extra pawn intact.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nxd7 {White might think 10.Nxd7 wins material, but it’s a mistake. We recapture with the queen, happy to trade more pieces and simplify while ahead.} 10...Qxd7 11.Bxf6 {White continues exchanging on f6, hoping to win the pawn back. We recapture with the g-pawn.} 11...gxf6 12.Rxf6 {White’s plan was to capture on f6 with the rook, but we’re one step ahead. We develop our bishop to g7, attacking the rook and slicing through the long diagonal.} 12...Bg7 13.Rf4 {The rook retreats to f4, trying to defend the d4 pawn and hold the position together.} 13...O-O-O {We castle long, completing development and connecting our rooks, with a borderline winning advantage. Our position is perfectly safe, our bishops dominate the board, and our h-rook will soon occupy the g-file. The d4-pawn is weak for now.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Everything Else! / Study 4: Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them (2. b3, 2. Bc4, 2.f4, etc) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d4f3504b-8bbd-4435-ae30-a0b8372c9286/chapters/cc854dbd-7422-4aa2-8ad3-41de27290268/studies/90246d3d-75eb-451f-b68d-696abfc79af4/lines"]

1.e4 {Let’s wrap things up by looking at all the odd White tries that don’t fit anywhere else. Even the wildest Caro deviations begin with 1.e4. As always, we reply with the solid Caro–Kann.} 1...c6 2.Nf3 {The move 2.Nf3 is played about 31% of the time, making it the second most popular choice. Wondering if White will go for the Two Knights? We simply continue our standard Caro routine with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.d3 {White plays the modest 3.d3, a timid setup that has recently gained some popularity. We switch gears and respond with 3...g6, a dynamic choice to fight for control of the long diagonal.} 3...g6 4.Nbd2 {White develops the other knight to d2, reinforcing the center. We complete our fianchetto, bringing the bishop to g7.} 4...Bg7 5.g3 {White follows suit with 5.g3, also going for a fianchetto since the d3 pawn blocks their bishop. We strike in the center with 5...e5, supported by our bishop on g7.} 5...e5 6.Bg2 {White finishes the fianchetto setup. We now bring our knight to e7, developing smoothly while keeping the bishop’s diagonal open.} 6...Ne7 7.O-O {White castles kingside, completing development. We castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.Re1 {After the rook comes to e1...} 8...d4 {We can close the center down, with strong control over the dark squares. The position is equal according to the engine, but we have a lot of space and a comfortable position from a human perspective.} *